; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; checker — the type half of the reference's internal/checker.
;
; ── What this module is FOR, and what a slice of it is ──────────────────────
;
; The checker answers two questions about a bound tree, and the fifth yardstick
; compares both (tests/oracle/types.go, TESTPLAN.md's *The fifth yardstick*):
;
;   C  the SEMANTIC DIAGNOSTICS of one file — GetDiagnostics, which runs the
;      whole check of the file and answers out of the checker's own sorted list
;   T  the TYPE of every node the reference's own baseline walker may ask about —
;      GetTypeAtLocation plus TypeToString
;
; Neither is reachable through any earlier artifact, which is why the yardstick is
; a fifth one rather than a section of the fourth: a type is not in the tree and
; not on a symbol. It is computed on demand, memoised inside the checker and never
; written back, so a port that computes NO types and a port that computes WRONG
; ones compare exactly equal on the first four, in both directions.
;
; ── The dimension's size, and what that decides ─────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ internal/checker is 59 971 lines against the 12 540 of scanner + parser +
; binder — measured at slice 46, which projects 88 000–141 000 lines of Scaly, two
; to three times the whole port to date. So this file will be built over many
; slices, and the SKELETON's job is to make every one of them an ARM: the type
; representation, the intrinsic types, the printer's keyword arms, the file-level
; check, the diagnostic list with the reference's sort, and — the part that earns
; its keep — a REPORT at every arm that is not ported, so tests/triage.py names
; the arm that unlocks the most units next. That is the binder's arrangement
; (§3.5bw) one dimension over.
;
; ── What is NOT ported, and why each omission is a decision ─────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FLOW GRAPH IS NOT PORTED, AND IT IS THE CHECKER THAT NEEDS IT. The
; binder's own header says so — it builds none, and *its reader is the checker* —
; and this file is that reader arriving. Two things depend on it. Reachability:
; checkSourceElementWorker begins with checkSourceElementUnreachable, which asks
; either NodeFlagsUnreachable (a flag the binder sets from currentFlow and this
; port does not set) or the node's FlowNode (which does not exist here), so a port
; without it cannot know that a statement is dead and would silently produce no
; TS7027. Narrowing: every identifier READ goes through getFlowTypeOfReference, so
; the T section of any expression that mentions a variable depends on it — and
; TS2454 *used before being assigned* is the corpus's second most common
; diagnostic at 6 462. Both are therefore reported rather than approximated: an
; approximated answer here is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, which is
; the class §3.5bw records for is_in_top_level_context.
;
; ★★ THE RELATER AND THE NODEBUILDER ARE NOT PORTED EITHER, and each is a
; dimension rather than a gap. relater.go (5 006 lines) is assignability, which
; even `var x: string = 1` needs; nodebuilderimpl.go (3 585) is the type PRINTER,
; of which this file ports the keyword arms only — the twenty lines of
; typeToTypeNodeHelper that answer an INTRINSIC — because those are the only type
; shapes anything here can produce.
;
; ★★★ AND THE COUPLING THE SKELETON MEASURED, because it decides how this
; dimension advances: THE C SECTION GATES THE T SECTION. One dump is one
; comparison, so a unit counts as matched only when both halves agree — and the C
; half is complete only when the check of EVERY statement in the unit is ported.
; Measured over the whole corpus: 7 300 of 17 810 units — 41 % — have an EMPTY C
; section, and not one of them can be claimed without porting the checks that PROVE
; it empty. So the
; 1 230 663 type answers of the corpus wait behind its 63 127 diagnostics, and the
; checker dimension advances at the speed of checkSourceElement. ★The escape hatch
; is named here rather than taken: the ORACLE's output would not change by one
; byte if the comparison were SPLIT into two yardsticks (C and T), because the two
; sections are already separate lines — it is compare.py, triage.py, run.sh and
; ctl.sh that would each gain a column (slice 46's rule: all four in one commit,
; or the first battery lies). That is a harness decision with a measured argument
; behind it, and it belongs to whichever slice finds the coupling is actually
; holding the type machinery back.
;
; ── What the harness fixes, and what our side therefore inherits ────────────
;
; The oracle builds a STUB program: no module resolution, the per-case
; @target/@lib/@module directives ignored, and the lib set fixed to lib.es5.d.ts
; (priced at slice 46 as a 3 % diagnostic delta against the reference's default
; closure). Two of those three are symmetric for free here, because this port has
; no program layer at all. The third is not: this side reads NO lib, so every
; global name is unresolved — which is invisible while no arm resolves a name, and
; is the first thing the slice that ports name resolution has to answer. It is
; written down here so that slice does not discover it as a diff.
;
; ★ compilerOptions: the harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation
; and nothing else, so every option this file would read is at its default —
; strictNullChecks false, exactOptionalPropertyTypes false, noImplicitAny false,
; NoUnusedLocals/NoUnusedParameters false (hence checkUnused false at
; getDiagnostics). Each place that matters says which option it is reading and
; what its default answers, so that an options record arriving later is a
; substitution rather than a search.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.String
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.binder.Binder
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList

; ── ObjectFlags: two bits, deliberately not a table ─────────────────────────
;
; ★★ The reference's ObjectFlags is a 40-entry uint32 enum in
; internal/checker/types.go, and it is NOT generated beside TypeFlags for a reason
; that is about the enum rather than about effort: its bit positions are REUSED.
; `1 << 22` is ObjectFlagsContainsSpread on a type with TypeFlagsObject,
; ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed on a union or intersection, and the comments in
; the source are what say which — so a flat table of names would read as 40
; independent bits and be wrong in exactly the way a reader cannot see. It also
; carries a FORWARD reference (ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask names
; ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType, declared below it), which gentypeflags.py
; would refuse rather than resolve.
;
; So the two bits the intrinsic block below actually SETS are declared here, with
; their positions from the source, and the table arrives with the slice that needs
; the object types — where the reuse discipline can be ported with it.
define ObjectFlagsNone: int 0
define ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType: int 1 << 16   ; Type is or contains undefined or null widening type
define ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType: int 1 << 18      ; Type is or contains anyFunctionType or silentNeverType

; ── Type ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `Type` is a tag struct plus an interface-typed payload
; (IntrinsicType, LiteralType, ObjectType, UnionType, …), which is the shape
; CLAUDE.md §3.1 turns into a sum type. This slice produces exactly ONE payload
; kind — the intrinsic — so the record carries the intrinsic's own field directly
; and the union arrives with the second payload, which is the first point at which
; a `choose` can distinguish anything.
;
; ★ `id` is not compared by the yardstick: the dump prints a type's STRING, never
; its identity. It is kept because the reference's ids are assigned in creation
; order and several of its caches are keyed on them, so a port that dropped the
; counter would have to invent one later at every such site.
define Type
(
    id: int
    flags: int
    object_flags: int

    ; IntrinsicType.intrinsicName. A static C string in every case the reference
    ; has: "any", "error", "unresolved", "intrinsic", "unknown", "undefined",
    ; "null", "string", "number", "bigint", "symbol", "void", "never", "object".
    intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char]
)
{
    init(i: int, f: int, of: int, nm: pointer[const_char])
    {
        set id: i
        set flags: f
        set object_flags: of
        set intrinsic_name: nm
    }
}

; ── SemanticMeaning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ast.SemanticMeaning: which of the three declaration SPACES a declaration
; declares into. Three bits, no complement, so a hand-port is the whole table.
define SemanticMeaningNone: int 0
define SemanticMeaningValue: int 1 << 0
define SemanticMeaningType: int 1 << 1
define SemanticMeaningNamespace: int 1 << 2
define SemanticMeaningAll: int SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType | SemanticMeaningNamespace

define Checker
(
    host: pointer[Page]
    file: pointer[AstNode]

    ; The bind. The checker asks it two questions in this slice — is this file a
    ; module, and does it own a symbol — and everything the type machinery will
    ; need (getSymbolOfDeclaration, the tables) comes through it.
    b: pointer[Binder]

    ; ★ THE RE-SCANNING SCANNER IS THE BINDER'S, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER
    ; THAN A SAVING. grammarErrorOnFirstToken reports at
    ; scanner.GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition, which the reference builds a FRESH
    ; scanner for; the binder ported that in slice 31 as
    ; `range_of_token_at_position` and wrote out why a shared scanner is the same
    ; answer — it carries no diagnostic sink, `skip_trivia_at` does not touch
    ; scanner state, and the language variant is set from the file name at
    ; construction. All three still hold when the sharer is one phase further on,
    ; and the binder is finished with it by the time a checker exists. A second
    ; scanner over the same text would be a second thing to keep in step.

    ; sourceFile.IsDeclarationFile, read by the checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements
    ; guard.
    is_declaration_file: bool

    ; ── the three compiler options the grammar checks read (slices 49, 50) ───
    ;
    ; ★★ THEY ARE FIELDS RATHER THAN LITERAL `false`s, and the difference is what
    ; a later reader sees. The harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and
    ; NoErrorTruncation and nothing else, so ExperimentalDecorators and
    ; VerbatimModuleSyntax are both unset and both of these are false — but a
    ; branch written as `if false` reads as dead code somebody should delete,
    ; while a branch behind a named option reads as the option it is waiting for.
    ; When an options record arrives, these two are the first substitution and the
    ; three reports behind them (grammar-legacy-accessor-decorators,
    ; node-can-be-decorated-parameter, grammar-verbatim-module-syntax) become the
    ; work list for it.
    ;
    ; ★ `c.legacyDecorators = c.compilerOptions.ExperimentalDecorators ==
    ; core.TSTrue` — the reference computes it once in NewChecker, and so does
    ; create() below.
    legacy_decorators: bool
    verbatim_module_syntax: bool

    ; ★ Slice 50's addition, and it is the one of the three whose branch is
    ; FULLY PORTED rather than reported. `shouldCheckErasableSyntax` is
    ; `ErasableSyntaxOnly.IsTrue() && !IsInJSFile(node)` and its consequence is a
    ; plain c.error on the node — no program, no resolver — so the arms of
    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration and checkExportAssignment that read it carry
    ; live code behind a false option instead of a report. That is the difference
    ; the note above is drawing: a report belongs where the MACHINERY is missing,
    ; not where the option merely happens to be off.
    erasable_syntax_only: bool

    ; c.diagnostics — the checker's OWN collection, which is neither the parse's
    ; nor the bind's. Appended through add_direct: the reference's addDiagnostic
    ; has no dedup (that is the PARSER's parseErrorAtRange), and its
    ; serializationLevel guard cannot fire here because nothing recurses into the
    ; printer.
    diagnostics: pointer[DiagnosticList]

    ; nextTypeId, in creation order.
    type_count: int

    ; ── the intrinsic types, in the reference's own creation order ───────────
    ;
    ; NewChecker builds these before anything else, and the ORDER is theirs
    ; because the ids are assigned along it. What is missing from the run is named
    ; at initialize_intrinsic_types.
    any_type: pointer[Type]
    auto_type: pointer[Type]
    wildcard_type: pointer[Type]
    blocked_string_type: pointer[Type]
    error_type: pointer[Type]
    unresolved_type: pointer[Type]
    non_inferrable_any_type: pointer[Type]
    intrinsic_marker_type: pointer[Type]
    unknown_type: pointer[Type]
    undefined_type: pointer[Type]
    undefined_widening_type: pointer[Type]
    missing_type: pointer[Type]
    undefined_or_missing_type: pointer[Type]
    optional_type: pointer[Type]
    null_type: pointer[Type]
    null_widening_type: pointer[Type]
    string_type: pointer[Type]
    number_type: pointer[Type]
    bigint_type: pointer[Type]
    es_symbol_type: pointer[Type]
    void_type: pointer[Type]
    never_type: pointer[Type]
    silent_never_type: pointer[Type]
    implicit_never_type: pointer[Type]
    unreachable_never_type: pointer[Type]
    non_primitive_type: pointer[Type]
    unique_literal_type: pointer[Type]

    ; c.nodeLinks[node].hasReportedStatementInAmbientContext, as a SET of the
    ; nodes that have it. The reference keeps a links record per node and this
    ; slice reads exactly one bit off it, so a set is the whole of it; the record
    ; arrives with the first arm that needs a second field.
    ;
    ; ★ Linear membership, deliberately. The set only ever holds nodes in an
    ; AMBIENT context that have already reported, which is a handful per file even
    ; in a `.d.ts` — and the reference's own map is keyed by pointer identity,
    ; which is what this compares.
    reported_ambient: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]

    ; The unported report, in the parser's and the binder's shape: the FIRST
    ; construct this slice does not implement, its tag, and a detail (a kind, in
    ; almost every case). The dump is suppressed once this is set — an incomplete
    ; port that answered a well-formed dump would be reporting agreement it has
    ; not earned.
    unported_pos: int
    unported_tag: pointer[const_char]
    unported_detail: int
)
{
    ; ── entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checker.NewChecker, minus the forty-method Program: the oracle's stub answers
    ; every resolution question with nothing, and this side has no program layer to
    ; answer them from either, so the two agree by having the same hole. See the
    ; header for what that costs and where it will first be visible.
    function create(host: pointer[Page], file: pointer[AstNode], file_name: String, b: pointer[Binder]) returns pointer[Checker]
    {
        let c host.allocate(sizeof Checker, alignof Checker) as pointer[Checker]
        set c.host: host
        set c.file: file
        set c.b: b
        set c.is_declaration_file: is_declaration_file_name(file_name)
        set c.legacy_decorators: false
        set c.verbatim_module_syntax: false
        set c.erasable_syntax_only: false
        let dl host.allocate(sizeof DiagnosticList, alignof DiagnosticList) as pointer[DiagnosticList]
        set *dl: DiagnosticList(host)
        set c.diagnostics: dl
        set c.type_count: 0
        let ra host.allocate(sizeof Array[pointer[AstNode]], alignof Array[pointer[AstNode]]) as pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]
        set *ra: Array[pointer[AstNode]]()
        set c.reported_ambient: ra
        set c.unported_pos: 0 - 1
        set c.unported_tag: null
        set c.unported_detail: 0
        c.initialize_intrinsic_types()
        c
    }

    ; ── the unported report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The binder's mechanism, verbatim in intent: the FIRST report wins, so the
    ; tag names the construct that stopped the check rather than the last one
    ; noticed. `pos` is 0 rather than a real position for the same reason the
    ; binder's is — the dump's consumer wants the TAG, and a checker report is
    ; about a construct rather than about a place in the text.
    procedure record_unported(this, tag: pointer[const_char], detail: int)
    {
        if unported_pos < 0
        {
            set unported_pos: 0
            set unported_tag: tag
            set unported_detail: detail
        }
    }

    function is_unported(this) returns bool
        unported_pos >= 0

    function unported_at(c: pointer[Checker]) returns int
        c.unported_pos

    function unported_tag_at(c: pointer[Checker]) returns pointer[const_char]
        c.unported_tag

    function unported_detail_at(c: pointer[Checker]) returns int
        c.unported_detail

    ; ── the intrinsic types ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; newType, reduced to what an intrinsic needs. The reference threads a payload
    ; interface through here; this slice has one payload kind (see Type's note).
    function new_intrinsic_type_ex(this, flags: int, intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char], object_flags: int) returns pointer[Type]
    {
        set type_count: type_count + 1
        let t host.allocate(sizeof Type, alignof Type) as pointer[Type]
        set *t: Type(type_count, flags, object_flags, intrinsic_name)
        t
    }

    function new_intrinsic_type(this, flags: int, intrinsic_name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[Type]
        this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(flags, intrinsic_name, ObjectFlagsNone)

    ; createWideningType. Its whole body is `if c.strictNullChecks { return
    ; nonWideningType }` and then a fresh intrinsic carrying
    ; ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType — and strictNullChecks is TRUE under this
    ; harness's options (see the header), so the FIRST branch is the one that runs.
    ; It is written as the reference's conditional anyway, with the option spelled
    ; as a constant, so an options record arriving later is a substitution.
    function create_widening_type(this, non_widening_type: pointer[Type]) returns pointer[Type]
    {
        if Checker.strict_null_checks()
            return non_widening_type
        this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(non_widening_type.flags, non_widening_type.intrinsic_name, ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType)
    }

    ; The two compilerOptions this slice reads, as functions rather than fields:
    ; there is no options record yet, and a field would claim one exists. Each
    ; answers the DEFAULT, which is what the oracle's stub program sets (it passes
    ; SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and nothing else).
    ;
    ; ★★★ strictNullChecks WAS `false` HERE FROM SLICE 47 TO SLICE 52 AND THE
    ; DEFAULT IS `true` — corrected in slice 52, and the shape of the mistake is
    ; worth more than the line. The two options are read through DIFFERENT
    ; accessors and only one of them takes the family default:
    ; `c.strictNullChecks = GetStrictOptionValue(options.StrictNullChecks)`, whose
    ; body is `if value != TSUnknown { return value == TSTrue }; return
    ; options.Strict != TSFalse` — so an UNSET option answers TRUE, because Strict
    ; is unset too and the test is `!= TSFalse` rather than `== TSTrue`. Meanwhile
    ; `c.exactOptionalPropertyTypes = options.ExactOptionalPropertyTypes ==
    ; core.TSTrue` is a plain equality and DOES answer false. Reading "the harness
    ; sets no options" as "every option is off" is therefore wrong for the four
    ; strict-family options (strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes, noImplicitAny,
    ; strictBindCallApply) and right for the rest — a third reading of "unset", next
    ; to the two check_signature_declaration meets (a FALSE default that kills a
    ; branch, and a target default at its MAXIMUM that kills a `<` comparison).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS UNOBSERVABLE, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED SIX SLICES, and the reason
    ; is worth writing down: its one reader is create_widening_type, and a wrong
    ; answer there does not produce a wrong TYPE — it produces two EXTRA types
    ; (a fresh widening intrinsic instead of the non-widening one returned
    ; unchanged), so the type COUNTER and every type ID after undefinedWideningType
    ; shift by two. The dump's T section prints `TypeToString(t)`, a NAME, and
    ; nothing in this suite compares an id or the counter. Correcting it leaves all
    ; five yardsticks byte-identical — which is the same fact from the other side,
    ; and is the checksum this correction carries instead of a gate.
    function strict_null_checks() returns bool
        true

    function exact_optional_property_types() returns bool
        false

    ; NewChecker's intrinsic block. The order is the reference's, because the type
    ; ids are assigned along it.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS MISSING FROM THE RUN, and each omission is one mechanism rather
    ; than one type: the BOOLEAN family (regularFalseType, falseType,
    ; regularTrueType, trueType) needs newLiteralType, i.e. a value payload on
    ; Type — §3.5be's rule says a field with no reader is DECLARED rather than
    ; ported, and nothing in this slice can produce a literal type, so the payload
    ; arrives with the arm that does. booleanType, stringOrNumberType,
    ; stringNumberSymbolType, numberOrBigIntType and templateConstraintType need
    ; getUnionType; numericStringType needs getTemplateLiteralType; and the
    ; fifteen empty/marker OBJECT types need newAnonymousType and newTypeParameter.
    ; Every one of them is reachable only from an arm that reports, so their
    ; absence cannot be observed — which is exactly why it is listed here instead.
    procedure initialize_intrinsic_types(this)
    {
        set any_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        set auto_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsAny, "any", ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType)
        set wildcard_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        set blocked_string_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
        ; ★ errorType's intrinsic NAME is "error" and its printed form is "any":
        ; the printer branches on TypeFlagsAny and never reads the name except to
        ; recognise intrinsicMarkerType. That is why a `T … any` line in the dump
        ; is most often this type rather than anyType — see get_type_of_node's
        ; fallthrough.
        set error_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "error")
        set unresolved_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "unresolved")
        set non_inferrable_any_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsAny, "any", ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType)
        set intrinsic_marker_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsAny, "intrinsic")
        set unknown_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUnknown, "unknown")
        set undefined_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        set undefined_widening_type: this.create_widening_type(undefined_type)
        set missing_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        ; core.IfElse(c.exactOptionalPropertyTypes, c.missingType, c.undefinedType)
        set undefined_or_missing_type: undefined_type
        if Checker.exact_optional_property_types()
            set undefined_or_missing_type: missing_type
        set optional_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsUndefined, "undefined")
        set null_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNull, "null")
        set null_widening_type: this.create_widening_type(null_type)
        set string_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsString, "string")
        set number_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNumber, "number")
        set bigint_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsBigInt, "bigint")
        set es_symbol_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsESSymbol, "symbol")
        set void_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsVoid, "void")
        set never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set silent_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type_ex(TypeFlagsNever, "never", ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType)
        set implicit_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set unreachable_never_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
        set non_primitive_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNonPrimitive, "object")
        ; Special `never` flagged by union reduction to behave as a literal.
        set unique_literal_type: this.new_intrinsic_type(TypeFlagsNever, "never")
    }

    ; ── the type printer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; TypeToString → typeToString → typeToStringEx → nodeBuilder.TypeToTypeNode →
    ; the emit printer. THIS PORTS THE KEYWORD ARMS OF THAT WORKER AND NOTHING
    ; ELSE, and the boundary is not arbitrary: those arms answer a bare keyword
    ; that the printer prints unchanged, so they are the shapes for which the
    ; nodebuilder and the printer are both identity. Every other arm builds a NODE
    ; and needs a printer, which is a dimension of its own (3 585 + 486 lines).
    ;
    ; ★ THE ORDER OF THE TESTS IS THE REFERENCE'S, and it is load-bearing rather
    ; than stylistic: the flags are not disjoint. A fresh boolean literal carries
    ; TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral, and an enum literal carries StringLiteral or
    ; NumberLiteral as well as EnumLiteral, so testing Void before the literal
    ; flags would answer a keyword for a literal type. Every arm this slice does
    ; not port therefore stands IN PLACE, reporting, rather than being left out.
    ;
    ; ★ Two things typeToStringEx does before the worker are no-ops here and say
    ; so at their line: the serializationLevel cutoff (nothing recurses into the
    ; printer, so the level is always 0) and the truncation (NoErrorTruncation is
    ; set by the harness, which is the whole reason the oracle passes it — a
    ; truncated name would make the T section depend on a display budget).
    ;
    ; Answers a static C string, which is what every ported arm produces. The arm
    ; that first needs composition is the one that widens this to a builder.
    function type_to_string(this, t: pointer[Type]) returns pointer[const_char]
    {
        if t = null
        {
            ; The reference PANICS here ("should always get typenode"), because a
            ; nil type reaches the worker only with FlagsAllowEmptyUnionOrIntersection
            ; set and typeToString does not set it. Reported rather than trapped:
            ; the dump's caller skips a nil type exactly as the oracle does, so
            ; this is unreachable from the walk and a report says so without
            ; inventing a keyword.
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-null", 0)
            return null
        }

        ; getReducedType, which the worker calls when FlagsNoTypeReduction is
        ; absent — as it is here. It is the identity on everything but a union or
        ; an intersection with a possibly-never member, and both of those report
        ; below, so the call is the identity for every type this slice can hold.
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsAny) <> 0
        {
            ; t.alias — the type-alias name a type may carry — is not represented
            ; yet, and cannot be: an alias is set by getTypeFromTypeNode, which
            ; reports. The arm stands here so the next reader sees where it goes.
            if t = unresolved_type
            {
                ; The reference wraps the keyword in a SYNTHETIC LEADING COMMENT
                ; ("/*unresolved*/ any") and prints it with a printer that keeps
                ; comments, where every other type is printed with one that
                ; removes them. That is a printer feature rather than a name, so
                ; it reports.
                this.record_unported("type-to-string-unresolved", 0)
                return null
            }
            if t = intrinsic_marker_type
                return "intrinsic"
            return "any"
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUnknown) <> 0
            return "unknown"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsString) <> 0
            return "string"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNumber) <> 0
            return "number"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBigInt) <> 0
            return "bigint"
        ; `t.flags&Boolean != 0 && t.alias == nil` — booleanType is a UNION of the
        ; two boolean literals, so this arm needs getUnionType before it can ever
        ; be entered, and no boolean type exists here to enter it with.
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBoolean) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-boolean", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsEnumLike) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-enum", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsStringLiteral) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-string-literal", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNumberLiteral) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-number-literal", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBigIntLiteral) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-bigint-literal", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsBooleanLiteral) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-boolean-literal", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUniqueESSymbol) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("type-to-string-unique-symbol", 0)
            return null
        }
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsVoid) <> 0
            return "void"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsUndefined) <> 0
            return "undefined"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNull) <> 0
            return "null"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNever) <> 0
            return "never"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsESSymbol) <> 0
            return "symbol"
        if (t.flags & TypeFlagsNonPrimitive) <> 0
            return "object"
        ; Everything past this point is the this-type, the alias, and the object /
        ; union / intersection / instantiable machinery — the rest of
        ; nodebuilderimpl.
        this.record_unported("type-to-string", t.flags)
        null
    }

    ; ── GetTypeAtLocation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getTypeOfNode, transcribed as a CHAIN because that is what it is: fifteen
    ; predicates in a fixed order, ending in errorType. A kind switch would be a
    ; different function — several of the predicates depend on a node's PARENT, not
    ; on its kind — so the shape is kept and the arms report.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE GATE IN THE MIDDLE IS THE HONEST PART. Ten of those predicates
    ; are not ported (IsExpressionNode, IsDeclarationNode, IsTypeDeclaration,
    ; IsTypeDeclarationName, IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName,
    ; IsBindingPattern, isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment, IsMetaProperty,
    ; IsImportAttributes, and the class-extends pair), and a chain that skipped a
    ; predicate it cannot evaluate would fall through to errorType and answer
    ; `any` — a well-formed WRONG answer, on the most common line the yardstick
    ; compares (`any` is 43 % of the corpus's 1 230 663 type answers, so a wrong
    ; `any` is the single easiest way for this port to look right). So a kind
    ; INVENTORY stands in front of them, listing the kinds for which every
    ; unported predicate is provably false; every other kind reports with its own
    ; number as the detail, which is the histogram tests/triage.py groups.
    function get_type_of_node(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns pointer[Type]
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        ; `ast.IsSourceFile(node) && !ast.IsExternalOrCommonJSModule(...)`. The
        ; walk never asks about the source file itself (both dumpers skip it), so
        ; this arm is unreachable from the yardstick and is ported because it is
        ; one line and because the next reader would otherwise have to prove that
        ; again.
        if k = KindSourceFile
        {
            if b.is_external_or_common_js_module() = false
                return error_type
        }

        ; "We cannot answer semantic questions within a with block, do not proceed
        ; any further" — the reference's own comment. The flag is the parser's, so
        ; this arm is complete.
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsInWithStatement) <> 0
            return error_type

        if Checker.kind_has_ported_type_arm(k) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("type-of-node", k)
            return null
        }

        ; The chain's own end: "if we get here, the node is not something we have
        ; a type for". Reached by exactly the kinds the inventory admits.
        error_type
    }

    ; ★★★ THE INVENTORY, AND WHAT ADMITTING A KIND COSTS. A kind belongs here only
    ; when every predicate of getTypeOfNode that this slice cannot evaluate is
    ; provably false for it — in EVERY context, since the predicates read parents.
    ; Slice 47 admits exactly one, and writes the proof out because the next slice
    ; will extend the list and needs the standard:
    ;
    ; KindEndOfFile (1). It is the SourceFile's endOfFileToken, so its parent is
    ; always the source file. IsPartOfTypeNode: the kind is outside
    ; [FirstTypeNode, LastTypeNode] and is in none of the switch's cases.
    ; IsExpressionNode: not in its list, and its default is false.
    ; TryGetClassImplementingOrExtendingExpressionWithTypeArguments requires an
    ; ExpressionWithTypeArguments. IsTypeDeclaration and IsDeclarationNode are
    ; properties of the node LAYOUT and the endOfFileToken is a plain token node.
    ; IsTypeDeclarationName and IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName both require
    ; the node to BE a name, i.e. an identifier or a literal.
    ; IsBindingElement / IsBindingPattern: the two binding kinds.
    ; isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment walks up from an entity NAME.
    ; IsMetaProperty(node.Parent) is false because the parent is the source file.
    ; IsImportAttributes is one kind. So the token reaches the fallthrough and its
    ; type is errorType, printed `any` — which is what the reference answers for
    ; it in every unit of the corpus.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 48 ADDS TWO, AND THE WAY IT EARNS THEM IS THE POINT. Slice 47's
    ; note here read *the statement kinds almost certainly belong, but "almost
    ; certainly" is not the standard, and the honest way to admit them is with
    ; IsDeclarationNode ported rather than with a second enumeration by hand*.
    ; It is ported now (DeclarationKinds.scaly, derived from the reference's own
    ; struct graph and verified against its own DeclarationData()), so
    ; `is_declaration` answers the tenth predicate instead of a paragraph, and
    ; that is what the two admissions below rest on.
    ;
    ; KindEmptyStatement (243) and KindDebuggerStatement (260). The proof is the
    ; end-of-file token's, re-run for a STATEMENT, and it must hold in every
    ; context — a `;` can be the body of an `if`, an arm of a case clause or a
    ; statement of a block, not only a top-level element:
    ;
    ;   IsPartOfTypeNode        ported, and answers false: neither kind is in
    ;                           [FirstTypeNode, LastTypeNode] nor in the switch.
    ;   IsDeclaration           PORTED as of this slice, and false: neither kind
    ;                           is among the fifty-six.
    ;   IsExpressionNode        the switch lists no statement kind and its default
    ;                           is false.
    ;   TryGetClassImplementing…  requires an ExpressionWithTypeArguments.
    ;   IsTypeDeclaration       a six-kind list plus the import/export type-only
    ;                           arms; a statement is in none of them.
    ;   IsTypeDeclarationName   requires KindIdentifier.
    ;   IsDeclarationNameOrImportPropertyName  either the parent is an
    ;                           import/export specifier and the node must then be
    ;                           an identifier or a string literal, or it reduces to
    ;                           IsDeclarationName, whose last test is
    ;                           `parent.Name() == node` — and a declaration's Name()
    ;                           is a name node, never a statement.
    ;   IsBindingElement / IsBindingPattern   three kinds, none of them these.
    ;   isInRightSideOfImportOrExportAssignment  the parent must be an
    ;                           ImportEqualsDeclaration whose ModuleReference this
    ;                           is, or an ExportAssignment whose Expression it is;
    ;                           both slots take an entity name or an expression.
    ;   IsMetaProperty(parent)  a MetaProperty's children are tokens.
    ;   IsImportAttributes      one kind.
    ;
    ; So both reach the fallthrough and answer errorType, printed `any` — which is
    ; what the reference answers for them in every unit of the corpus, and what
    ; `checker_ambient_statements_only.d.ts` is there to hold it to.
    function kind_has_ported_type_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindEndOfFile
            return true
        if k = KindEmptyStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDebuggerStatement
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ── ast.GetMeaningFromDeclaration ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ IT LIVES HERE AND UPSTREAM HAS IT IN internal/ast, and the reason is this
    ; port's own layout rather than a preference: its ModuleDeclaration arm asks
    ; GetModuleInstanceState, which the reference keeps in `ast` and slice 31 put on
    ; the BINDER (it needs the visited set and the ancestor chain that the binder
    ; carries). The `ast` module is declared before `binder`, so a function in
    ; ast.scaly could not reach it — a module-level name from a module declared
    ; LATER is a hard `unknown identifier` and, in call-argument position, a
    ; silently dropped call. So the one arm that needs the binder decides where the
    ; whole function goes, and it goes with its dependency.
    ;
    ; Its reader is the type walk's exclusion for an IDENTIFIER: a name that
    ; declares nothing into the VALUE space is not asked for its type, with the
    ; type-alias name as the one exception.
    function get_meaning_from_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if node = null
            return SemanticMeaningAll
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)

        if Checker.kind_declares_a_value_only(k)
            return SemanticMeaningValue
        if Checker.kind_declares_a_type_only(k)
            return SemanticMeaningType
        if k = KindEnumMember
            return SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType

        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if Binder.is_ambient_module(node)
                return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue
            if b.get_module_instance_state(node) = ModuleInstanceStateInstantiated
                return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue
            return SemanticMeaningNamespace
        }

        ; The reference's list here returns SemanticMeaningAll, which is also the
        ; function's default — the kinds are enumerated upstream to say that the
        ; answer is deliberate, and the enumeration changes no answer, so it is a
        ; comment here rather than eight more comparisons: EnumDeclaration,
        ; NamedImports, ImportSpecifier, ImportEqualsDeclaration, ImportDeclaration,
        ; JSImportDeclaration, ExportAssignment, ExportDeclaration. ★ And the
        ; SourceFile arm — "an external module can be a Value" — answers
        ; Namespace|Value, which IS different from the default and is therefore
        ; below rather than in this comment.
        if k = KindSourceFile
            return SemanticMeaningNamespace | SemanticMeaningValue

        SemanticMeaningAll
    }

    ; The two long `case` lists of GetMeaningFromDeclaration, as functions for the
    ; reason ast.scaly's is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node is one.
    function kind_declares_a_value_only(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        if k = KindBindingElement
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if k = KindCatchClause
            return true
        if k = KindJsxAttribute
            return true
        false
    }

    function kind_declares_a_type_only(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeLiteral
            return true
        false
    }

    ; checker.IsTypeAny, which the type walk's class-extends workaround asks: the
    ; reference re-asks the node itself when the parent's answer is `any`.
    function is_type_any(t: pointer[Type]) returns bool
    {
        if t = null
            return false
        (t.flags & TypeFlagsAny) <> 0
    }

    ; ── GetDiagnostics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getDiagnostics: checkUnused is false under this harness's options (see the
    ; header), so the whole suggestion/unused branch is not entered, and what is
    ; left is checkSourceFile followed by the collection's sorted view.
    procedure get_diagnostics(this)
    {
        this.check_source_file()
        ; GetDiagnosticsForFile sorts the collection before answering — see
        ; DiagnosticList.sort_by_position for the comparison and for why this list
        ; sorts while the parser's does not.
        diagnostics.sort_by_position()
    }

    ; checkSourceFile. The `links.typeChecked` once-guard is dropped for the
    ; binder's reason: a Checker here is one object, used once, on the caller's
    ; page.
    procedure check_source_file(this)
    {
        ; Grammar checking. The result is discarded upstream too — the bool is
        ; there for the `&&` inside checkGrammarSourceFile and nothing reads it.
        this.check_grammar_source_file()

        ; `c.renamedBindingElementsInTypes = nil` — the list
        ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration appends to and
        ; checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements reads. Neither end is ported, so there
        ; is nothing here to clear; the two are named together at the read below.

        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.statements_of(file))
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        this.check_deferred_nodes()
        if this.is_unported()
            return

        if b.is_external_or_common_js_module()
        {
            ; checkExternalModuleExports plus registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck.
            ; The second is observable only through checkUnusedIdentifiers, which
            ; checkUnused gates off here, so what this arm owes is the first: the
            ; module's export table walked for a name exported twice with
            ; different meanings. It needs the module symbol's exports and
            ; getSymbolFlags, so it reports — and note it is reached only AFTER
            ; every statement has been checked, which is why a module's dump is
            ; usually stopped by one of its statements rather than by this line.
            this.record_unported("check-external-module-exports", 0)
            return
        }

        if is_declaration_file = false
            this.check_unused_renamed_binding_elements()

        this.produce_deferred_diagnostics()
    }

    procedure check_source_elements(this, nodes: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]])
    {
        if nodes = null
            return
        var i 0
        while i < AstNode.list_count(nodes)
        {
            ; ★★★ SLICE 47 STOPPED HERE ON A REPORT AND SLICE 49 DOES NOT, AND THE
            ; REASON IT GAVE IS WHY. It read *the reference breaks on cancellation;
            ; a report is this port's equivalent stopping condition and there is
            ; nothing to gain from checking the rest of a file whose dump is
            ; already suppressed* — true on the day it was written, and slice 48
            ; built the instrument that makes it false: tests/diagcheck.sh compares
            ; the C section of every unit, suppressed dump or not, so the
            ; statements after the first report are exactly where a wrong span or
            ; an invented diagnostic would hide. It is also the more faithful
            ; reading: the reference checks every element, and cancellation is not
            ; a thing this port has.
            ;
            ; ★ The relation diagcheck asserts survives the change and gets
            ; stronger. With the stop, our lines were a PREFIX of the reference's;
            ; without it they are a SUBSET, and both sides are sorted, so a
            ; subsequence test holds either way — over more of the file.
            ; §3.5cp's lesson, one dimension on: a deferral's REASON expires, and
            ; nothing re-reads it when it does.
            this.check_source_element(AstNode.child_in_list(nodes, i))
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; checkSourceElement's whole body is the save/restore of two registers around
    ; checkSourceElementWorker plus `c.instantiationCount = 0`. currentNode is read
    ; by the instantiation-depth machinery and withinUnreachableCode by the
    ; reachability check — neither of which exists here, for the reason the header
    ; gives — so the saves have nothing to save yet and the worker is called
    ; directly. The function is kept because every arm of the checker calls it and
    ; the registers arrive with the flow graph.
    procedure check_source_element(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if node = null
            return
        this.check_source_element_worker(node)
    }

    ; ★★★ checkSourceElementWorker. NINETEEN ARMS OF ITS SIXTY ARE PORTED (three
    ; in slice 47, five in slice 49, eight in slice 50 — the import/export/module
    ; family plus the two blocks — the class in slice 51, the function declaration
    ; in slice 52 and the variable DECLARATION in slice 54; slice 53 added none and
    ; finished one of the eighteen instead). checkVariableStatement runs to its last
    ; line, so the VariableStatement arm is the third in this file that is COMPLETE
    ; rather than a prefix — the count is a count of arms REACHED, not of arms
    ; finished, and those are two different numbers.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SINCE SLICE 54 THE COUNT HAS A THIRD CATEGORY BESIDE `ported` AND `not
    ; ported`: the twelve kinds the switch has NO CASE for (kind_has_no_check_arm).
    ; They are not arms and they are not holes — doing nothing IS the reference's
    ; answer — and they are the reason the gate below can say true for a kind this
    ; file has no code for at all.
    ;
    ; What had to be settled before ANY of them
    ; could be was the two steps that stand in FRONT of the switch and run for
    ; every node. ★The count is the one kind_has_ported_check_arm answers true
    ; for, counted rather than incremented: it read *fifteen … seven in slice 50*
    ; for two slices while the table held sixteen and then seventeen, because the
    ; slice-50 figure was one short of its own parenthetical (six arms plus two
    ; blocks) and nothing re-added the sum.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REACHABILITY CHECK PRODUCES NOTHING THIS YARDSTICK COMPARES, and
    ; that is read off the reference rather than hoped: checkSourceElementUnreachable
    ; ends in `c.addErrorOrSuggestion(c.compilerOptions.AllowUnreachableCode ==
    ; core.TSFalse, diagnostic)`, and AllowUnreachableCode is UNSET under this
    ; harness (the oracle passes SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and
    ; nothing else), so the flag is TSUnknown, `== TSFalse` is false, and the
    ; diagnostic goes to `c.suggestionDiagnostics` with CategorySuggestion. The
    ; oracle calls GetDiagnostics, which reads `c.diagnostics`. Its only other
    ; effect is `c.withinUnreachableCode`, whose three readers are the guard
    ; itself and the save/restore around it. So TS7027 cannot appear in a C
    ; section at all here — which is why an arm may be claimed without the flow
    ; graph, and why this is stated at the line rather than left as a hole. ★It is
    ; conditional on the OPTION: a slice that lets `@allowUnreachableCode: false`
    ; through would make the whole family observable and owe the flow graph.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE JSDOC PASS IS GATED ON THE FLAG THE REFERENCE GATES IT ON.
    ; `node.EagerJSDoc(nil)` answers nil unless NodeFlagsHasJSDoc is set, so a node
    ; without the flag cannot enter the loop and the step is a no-op we can prove
    ; rather than assume. With the flag set it reports: checkJSDocComment acts only
    ; on the three @link kinds and resolves with ignoreErrors, so it very probably
    ; adds nothing either — but *very probably* is not the standard the kind
    ; inventory below is held to, and resolveJSDocMemberName reaches
    ; getTypeOfSymbol, which reports at ITS own sites. The tighter gate (walk the
    ; comment parts and admit a JSDoc that carries no link) is a refinement with a
    ; measurement attached, not a guess.
    ;
    ; ★ The kind test comes FIRST, before both, and the order is ours rather than
    ; the reference's: for a kind whose arm is not ported the unit is unported
    ; either way, and the tag should name what actually blocks it. Putting the
    ; JSDoc gate in front would relabel a VariableStatement carrying a doc comment
    ; as `check-jsdoc-comments`, which fragments the histogram this column exists
    ; to be.
    procedure check_source_element_worker(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_has_ported_check_arm(k) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("check", k)
            this.spend_ambient_report_of_container(node)
            return
        }
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsHasJSDoc) <> 0
        {
            this.record_unported("check-jsdoc-comments", k)
            this.spend_ambient_report_of_container(node)
            return
        }
        ; The kinds the reference's switch has no CASE for, which is a different
        ; fact from an arm this port has not written — see kind_has_no_check_arm.
        ; The JSDoc gate stays in FRONT of it: the two steps before the switch run
        ; for every kind, and a keyword type carrying a doc comment would reach
        ; checkJSDocComments there as any other node does.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_check_arm(k)
            return

        ; case ast.KindBlock, ast.KindModuleBlock: c.checkBlock(node)
        ;
        ; ★★★ THIS ARM IS WHAT LIFTS SLICE 49's LAST BOUND. It wrote *only
        ; TOP-LEVEL statements are ever checked … anything inside a namespace body
        ; is reached through checkModuleDeclaration -> checkSourceElement(body) ->
        ; checkBlock, and neither arm is ported*, and named the next slice as the
        ; one that lifts it. Both arms are here, and they had to arrive together:
        ; the module arm's FIRST step is checkSourceElement(body), so porting it
        ; without this one would have measured the module prefix on a body that
        ; reports immediately.
        if k = KindBlock
        {
            this.check_block(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindModuleBlock
        {
            this.check_block(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindVariableStatement
        {
            this.check_variable_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
        {
            this.check_expression_statement(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
        {
            this.check_interface_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
        {
            this.check_type_alias_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
        {
            this.check_type_alias_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
        {
            this.check_enum_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 50 — the six arms whose head is checkGrammarModuleElementContext.
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            this.check_module_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
        {
            this.check_import_equals_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
        {
            this.check_export_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        if k = KindExportAssignment
        {
            this.check_export_assignment(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 51 — the class declaration, whose head is
        ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration.
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
        {
            this.check_class_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 52 — the function declaration, whose head is
        ; checkSignatureDeclaration.
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
        {
            this.check_function_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; Slice 54 — the variable declaration, which the VariableStatement arm's
        ; own walk reaches.
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            this.check_variable_declaration(node)
            return
        }
        ; case ast.KindEmptyStatement:    c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
        ; case ast.KindDebuggerStatement: c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
        ;
        ; Two arms whose whole body is that one call — so they are COMPLETE, which
        ; is the first time anything in this file can say so.
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
    }

    ; ★★★ THE ONCE-BIT OF checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext IS A PROPERTY OF
    ; THE WHOLE BLOCK, AND AN UNPORTED ARM MAKES ITS OWNER UNKNOWABLE. Slice 50
    ; found this the moment it ported the BLOCK arm, on
    ; `compiler_ambientWithStatements`: `declare namespace M { break; continue;
    ; debugger; … }` — the reference reports ONE TS1036, on the FIRST statement, and
    ; our port reported it on the `debugger`, because that is the first statement
    ; whose arm exists. A diagnostic at a position the reference does not have is
    ; the one thing diagcheck can fail on, and it did.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SO THE HONEST ANSWER IS TO SAY NOTHING, and the reason is exactly the
    ; reason the port reports rather than approximates everywhere else: the
    ; reference calls checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext from the FIRST LINE of
    ; some twenty statement arms (break/continue, return, if, do, while, for, throw,
    ; try, switch, with, …), so a statement whose arm this slice does not implement
    ; MIGHT have spent the bit. Marking the container is the only claim that cannot
    ; be wrong: it makes our C section a strict SUBSET of the reference's, which is
    ; the relation the instrument asserts, instead of a line in the wrong place.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT USES THE reported_ambient SET AND MEANS SOMETHING ELSE BY IT — *this
    ; container's report is spent or unknowable*, not *we reported it* — which is
    ; why it is its own procedure with its own name rather than a bare
    ; mark_reported_ambient at two call sites.
    ;
    ; ★ THE SUCCESSOR IS NAMED: porting the first line of those twenty arms puts the
    ; report back where the reference puts it and retires this function. Until then
    ; the number it costs is visible — it is read off diagcheck's *units we speak
    ; on*, which is what that counter is printed for.
    ;
    ; ★ Only an AMBIENT node can spend the bit (the check returns at its own first
    ; line otherwise), and only the in-block half of it is a property of the
    ; CONTAINER: the signature half marks the node itself, so an unported statement
    ; there can affect nothing else.
    procedure spend_ambient_report_of_container(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        var in_block false
        if pk = KindBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_block: true
        if in_block = false
            return
        if this.has_reported_ambient(parent)
            return
        this.mark_reported_ambient(parent)
    }

    function kind_has_ported_check_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        ; ★★★ THE KINDS WITH NO ARM AT ALL ANSWER TRUE HERE, and the word `ported`
        ; is what makes that look wrong: what this gate decides is whether the port
        ; SPEAKS on the kind, and for a kind the reference's switch does not
        ; mention, doing nothing IS the whole arm. Slice 54 needed the distinction
        ; because it is the first slice whose walk reaches a TYPE node.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_check_arm(k)
            return true
        if k = KindExpressionStatement
            return true
        if k = KindEmptyStatement
            return true
        if k = KindDebuggerStatement
            return true
        ; Slice 49. The five kinds whose arm BEGINS with checkGrammarModifiers and
        ; has nothing in front of it — so the ported prefix is the whole of the
        ; arm up to the first thing this slice does not implement, and the report
        ; names that thing.
        if k = KindVariableStatement
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 50. The BLOCK pair plus the six arms of the import/export/module
        ; family — the whole set that reaches checkGrammarModuleElementContext, and
        ; the two blocks that carry the statements those arms recurse into.
        if k = KindBlock
            return true
        if k = KindModuleBlock
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        ; Slice 51. The class declaration — one arm, three functions of prefix,
        ; and the first reader of a list's RANGE outside the parser.
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 52. The function declaration — the last of the switch's arms to be
        ; a HEAD, and the first whose grammar `||` this port runs to its end.
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        ; Slice 54. The variable declaration, reached by the VariableStatement
        ; arm's own walk through checkVariableDeclarationList.
        if k = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ★★★ THE KINDS checkSourceElementWorker's SWITCH HAS NO CASE FOR — so
    ; `checkSourceElement(x)` on one of them runs the two steps in front of the
    ; switch and then does NOTHING, and a report would be a claim that the unit is
    ; unmeasured where the reference measures nothing either. That is §3.5cx's
    ; checkExternalEmitHelpers argument at a place where it costs units rather than
    ; a comment: over stage 1 the three commonest annotations alone —
    ; `any` 35, `number` 16, `string` 14 — plus `void` 5 and a literal type 2 are
    ; **72 units** that would have carried a tag naming a function that does not
    ; exist. Measured on the way in, with the family switched off.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE REFERENCE'S SWITCH AND IT IS ENUMERABLE, which is
    ; what separates it from a guess: the twelve kinds below are the ELEVEN keyword
    ; types a type annotation can be — the ten of the parser's own
    ; `keyword_type_start` table, plus `void`, which that table deliberately omits
    ; because there is no `void.Foo` fallback, plus `intrinsic`, which only a type
    ; alias's right-hand side can be — and the LITERAL TYPE wrapper. Every OTHER
    ; kind a type annotation can be HAS a case (TypeReference, ArrayType,
    ; TupleType, UnionType, TypeLiteral, FunctionType, TypeOperator, MappedType, …
    ; twenty-six of them), so those report and the report is honest.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE LIST, because the list is only worth what
    ; its argument is: every other kind the switch omits — an EXPRESSION kind, a
    ; token, a JSX kind. Those are no-arm kinds too, but nothing in this port hands
    ; one to checkSourceElement, so admitting them would be a claim no instrument
    ; here can reach. The list grows with the arm that first walks into such a
    ; position.
    ;
    ; ★ A LiteralType's CHILD is not walked, and that is the reference's answer
    ; rather than an omission: no case means no ForEachChild, so `var x: "a"` never
    ; reaches the string literal. The three kinds whose case IS a bare
    ; `node.ForEachChild(c.checkSourceElement)` — Parenthesized/Optional/Rest — are
    ; not here for exactly that reason.
    function kind_has_no_check_arm(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindAnyKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUnknownKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindStringKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNumberKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBigIntKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindSymbolKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBooleanKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUndefinedKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNeverKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindObjectKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindVoidKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindIntrinsicKeyword
            return true
        k = KindLiteralType
    }

    ; checkExpressionStatement, whole:
    ;
    ;   c.checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext(node)
    ;   c.checkExpression(node.Expression())
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SECOND LINE IS THE CHECKER'S WHOLE EXPRESSION DIMENSION, and the
    ; report is placed there rather than on the statement so that the unported
    ; column stops saying `ExpressionStatement` — a kind nobody can act on — and
    ; starts naming the EXPRESSION kinds the corpus actually holds. That is the
    ; same move slice 47 made when it put the kind on `check`: this column is the
    ; work list, and a work list is only as useful as its resolution.
    procedure check_expression_statement(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if e = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-expression", AstNode.kind_of(e))
    }

    ; ── slice 49: the four arms whose first grammar check is the modifier one ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THESE FIVE KINDS AND NOT THE FOUR BIGGEST. The histogram's head is
    ; VariableStatement 3 940 / ClassDeclaration 3 022 / FunctionDeclaration 2 255
    ; / ImportDeclaration 1 601 on stage 2, and only the first of those is here.
    ; The other three do NOT begin with checkGrammarModifiers: a class reaches it
    ; through checkClassLikeDeclaration, whose own first line is
    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration; a function reaches it through
    ; checkFunctionOrMethodDeclaration → checkSignatureDeclaration; and the
    ; import/export family reaches it through checkGrammarModuleElementContext,
    ; which may RETURN before it. Each of those is a second head with its own
    ; prefix, and porting one of them without its prefix would run this function
    ; where the reference does not.
    ;
    ; ★★ The import/export family had a second reason to wait, and it was the
    ; generator's: their prefix reports messages that live in checker.go, which
    ; tools/gendiagnostics.py deliberately did not scrape (some 1 000 messages,
    ; i.e. most of the table). The five kinds here report only through
    ; grammarchecks.go, which slice 48 added — so the boundary of SLICE 49 was the
    ; boundary of the message table it already had, a checkable line rather than a
    ; judgement. ★SLICE 50 PAID THAT BILL: checker.go joined the SOURCES on the
    ; condition the generator's own note set, the table went 337 -> 947 codes, and
    ; the family is ported below. This paragraph is kept in the past tense rather
    ; than deleted, because the argument it makes — let the generator's stated
    ; boundary decide the slice's — is the transferable half.

    ; checkVariableStatement, whole:
    ;
    ;   if !c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && !c.checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList(list) {
    ;       c.checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement(varStatement)
    ;   }
    ;   c.checkVariableDeclarationList(declarationList)
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 53 RUNS IT TO THE END, AND WHAT THAT BUYS IS NOT DIAGNOSTICS — IT
    ; IS THE WALK. The last line recurses into every declaration of the list, so
    ; the 276 stage-1 units this arm held move off `check-grammar-variable-
    ; declaration-list VariableStatement` and onto whatever the DECLARATIONS
    ; report, which is the histogram moving one level down exactly as slice 50's
    ; block arm and slice 52's parameter list moved it.
    ;
    ; ★ The `&&` is a SHORT-CIRCUIT and both terms are real now, so the old
    ; two-tag report is gone with them. What is left unported inside the chain is
    ; one term of checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList (the await-using tail) and
    ; nothing else; the block-scoped statement check below is complete.
    procedure check_variable_statement(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        let list AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if this.check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(list) = false
                this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_block_scoped_variable_statement(node)
        }
        this.check_variable_declaration_list(list)
    }

    ; checkVariableDeclarationList, whole:
    ;
    ;   blockScopeKind := ast.GetCombinedNodeFlags(node) & ast.NodeFlagsBlockScoped
    ;   if (blockScopeKind == Using || blockScopeKind == AwaitUsing) &&
    ;       c.languageVersion < LanguageFeatureMinimumTarget.UsingAndAwaitUsing {
    ;       c.checkExternalEmitHelpers(node, …AddDisposableResourceAndDisposeResources)
    ;   }
    ;   c.checkSourceElements(node.AsVariableDeclarationList().Declarations.Nodes)
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EMIT-HELPERS CALL IS REACHED AND IS A PROVABLE NO-OP, WHICH IS A
    ; FOURTH READING OF AN UNSET OPTION TO SET BESIDE §3.5cw's THREE. The gate is
    ; TAKEN for a `using` declaration — `c.languageVersion` defaults to
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 = 12 and the feature's minimum is
    ; ScriptTargetESNext = 99, so `12 < 99` is true, which is the OPPOSITE
    ; direction to the two `languageVersion` reads slice 52 measured. What kills it
    ; is the callee's own first line: `if !c.compilerOptions.ImportHelpers.IsTrue()
    ; { return }`, and `IsTrue()` is `== TSTrue`, so an unset option answers false
    ; and checkExternalEmitHelpers returns before it looks at anything. So the
    ; whole family — resolveHelpersModule, getExportsOfModule, the per-helper name
    ; resolution — is unreachable under this harness, and the step is written as a
    ; comment with its argument rather than as a report, because a report would
    ; claim the unit is unmeasured when the reference does nothing either.
    ;
    ; ★ `GetCombinedNodeFlags` is `Binder.combined_node_flags`; the reference's
    ; caller here uses the UNCACHED spelling while the block-scoped check below
    ; uses `getCombinedNodeFlagsCached`, which is a one-entry memo of the same
    ; function — so the two are one call here, as the two spellings are one answer
    ; there.
    procedure check_variable_declaration_list(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if node = null
            return
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.variable_declarations_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 54: the variable DECLARATION ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkVariableDeclaration, whole: two calls, and the tracer above them is
    ; nothing here (the reference builds a tracing span; `c.tracer` is nil under
    ; this harness and the whole `if` is a no-op).
    procedure check_variable_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_variable_declaration(node)
        this.check_variable_like_declaration(node)
    }

    ; checkVariableLikeDeclaration's PREFIX — the shared arm of a variable
    ; declaration, a parameter, a property and a binding element, of which only the
    ; first reaches it in this slice.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THIS BUYS IS THE TYPE WALK, and it is the move §3.5cx predicted:
    ; `c.checkSourceElement(typeNode)` sends the histogram's biggest row one level
    ; down onto the TYPE kinds, exactly as the block arm and the parameter list did
    ; for statements. It also sends a destructuring declaration's ELEMENTS down, so
    ; `check BindingElement` becomes a row of its own.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THAT IS WHY THE NO-ARM FAMILY HAD TO ARRIVE WITH IT (see
    ; kind_has_no_check_arm). A keyword type is not a kind whose arm is unported:
    ; the reference's switch has NO case for it, so `checkSourceElement(number)`
    ; does nothing at all. Reporting `check NumberKeyword` would be §3.5cx's
    ; checkExternalEmitHelpers mistake with the sign flipped — a claim that the
    ; unit is unmeasured where the reference measures nothing either — and it would
    ; be the commonest row in the histogram.
    ;
    ; ★★ THREE OF THE REFERENCE'S BRANCHES ARE UNREACHABLE FOR THIS ONE KIND, and
    ; each is a comment rather than a report because the kind decides it:
    ; `IsBindingElement(node)` is false twice over (so the type walk RUNS and the
    ; binding-element block is skipped), `IsComputedPropertyName(name)` cannot hold
    ; — a variable declaration's name is an identifier or a binding pattern — and
    ; `IsPartOfParameterDeclaration(node)` is false, which kills the
    ; parameter-initializer report. All three become live with the Parameter and
    ; BindingElement arms, which is when they get their fixtures.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AMBIENT RETURN IS THE ONE PLACE THIS ARM COMPLETES. For a binding
    ; pattern in an ambient or type context the reference returns with nothing more
    ; to say, so a `declare namespace N { const { a } = x }` runs to the end of the
    ; arm — while the same declaration outside one needs the widened type and stops.
    procedure check_variable_like_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_decorators(node)
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if name = null
            return
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        this.check_source_element(type_node)
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
        {
            this.check_source_elements(AstNode.elements_of(name))
            if this.is_in_ambient_or_type_node(node)
                return
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            var grandparent null as pointer[AstNode]
            if parent <> null
                set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
            var need_check_initializer false
            if initializer <> null
            {
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(grandparent) <> KindForInStatement
                    set need_check_initializer: true
            }
            ; !core.Some(name.Elements(), n => n.Name() != nil) — an OmittedExpression
            ; has no name, so a pattern of nothing but holes needs the widened type.
            let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
            var need_check_widened_type true
            var i 0
            let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
            while i < n
            {
                if AstNode.name_of(AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)) <> null
                {
                    set need_check_widened_type: false
                    break
                }
                set i: i + 1
            }
            if need_check_initializer or need_check_widened_type
                this.record_unported("get-widened-type-for-variable-like-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("get-symbol-of-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; isInAmbientOrTypeNode, whole. FindAncestor with a three-kind predicate is
    ; written out as the walk it is (§3.9d) — and it starts AT the node, as
    ; FindAncestor does, which matters for a binding pattern that IS inside a type
    ; literal's member.
    function is_in_ambient_or_type_node(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            return true
        var p node
        while p <> null
        {
            let k AstNode.kind_of(p)
            if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return true
            if k = KindTypeLiteral
                return true
            set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkInterfaceDeclaration's first two lines:
    ;
    ;   if !c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) { c.checkGrammarInterfaceDeclaration(...) }
    ;   if !c.containerAllowsBlockScopedVariable(node.Parent) { c.grammarErrorOnNode(...) }
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SECOND LINE'S BLOCKER EXPIRED IN SLICE 53 and the report is kept on
    ; purpose, which is the opposite of §3.5cp's lesson and needs its own reason.
    ; containerAllowsBlockScopedVariable exists now, so this line is one statement
    ; away — but it is only reached on the MODIFIER-ERROR path, which the corpus
    ; takes **6 times in 17 855 units** (this tag's whole stage-2 row; stage 1 has
    ; none), and porting it would still leave the arm behind its FIRST blocker,
    ; checkGrammarInterfaceDeclaration. So it goes on the work list rather than
    ; into this function, DATED, so that the interface slice does not have to
    ; re-derive that the line is free — and the 6 is written down because *the path
    ; is empty* was the first guess and it was wrong.
    procedure check_interface_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        {
            this.record_unported("check-container-allows-block-scoped-variable", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("check-grammar-interface-declaration", KindInterfaceDeclaration)
    }

    ; checkTypeAliasDeclaration's first line. The arm serves both
    ; TypeAliasDeclaration and JSTypeAliasDeclaration, as the reference's switch
    ; does — one `case` with two kinds.
    procedure check_type_alias_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        this.record_unported("check-type-name-is-reserved", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkEnumDeclaration's first line. Unlike the two above, the result is
    ; DISCARDED here — the reference writes `c.checkGrammarModifiers(node)` on its
    ; own line — so there is one continuation and one tag.
    procedure check_enum_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        this.record_unported("check-collisions-for-declaration-name", KindEnumDeclaration)
    }

    ; ── slice 50: the import/export/module family, and the block it needed ───
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS ONE HEAD AND THE ARMS IT PREFIXES, and the line it stops at
    ; is a FILE rather than a judgement — the same shape slice 49's boundary had,
    ; where the message table decided. Everything these arms call in
    ; grammarchecks.go is ported (the head, checkGrammarModifiers, the export
    ; declaration's type-only check); the first call into a CHECK of checker.go is
    ; where each arm reports. The two exceptions are named because they look like
    ; the line being crossed and are not: `c.error` is a REPORTER, the semantic
    ; twin of grammar_error_on_node, and `shouldCheckErasableSyntax` is a
    ; one-line option read. Neither is a check.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE FIRST THING THIS SLICE HAD TO PAY IS THE MESSAGE TABLE. Slice 49
    ; recorded that this family *is the only one that also needs
    ; gendiagnostics.py's SOURCES to grow*: every one of the six arms names its
    ; illegal-context message in checker.go, which the generator deliberately did
    ; not scrape. It scrapes it now — 337 codes to 947 — on the condition the old
    ; note itself set (*it joins when an arm of checker.go reports*), and widening
    ; it found a defect in the scrape that only that file could show. The
    ; generator's header has it.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE FAMILY CANNOT DO YET, said plainly: the illegal-context report is
    ; UNREACHABLE. Its condition is a parent that is none of SourceFile,
    ; ModuleBlock or ModuleDeclaration — a `{ import … }` inside a plain block, a
    ; `export = x` inside an `if` body — and while checkBlock is ported for a
    ; block that is a STATEMENT, no arm that CONTAINS a block is (checkIfStatement,
    ; checkFunctionDeclaration, …). So the head runs on every unit of the family
    ; and answers false on every one of them. That is worth a fixture the moment a
    ; containing arm lands, and it is why the corpus is the evidence for the claim
    ; rather than a reading of it.

    ; checkBlock, whole. Two of its three steps are no-ops here for reasons the
    ; file already carries: `flowAnalysisDisabled` is saved and restored around a
    ; call, and there is no flow graph to disable (see the header); and
    ; registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck is observable only through
    ; checkUnusedIdentifiers, which checkUnused gates off under this harness.
    ;
    ; ★ The grammar check is asked of a KindBlock and NOT of a KindModuleBlock —
    ; that is the reference's own `if node.Kind == ast.KindBlock`, and it is the
    ; whole difference between the two kinds here. A namespace body is not a
    ; statement, so *statements are not allowed in ambient contexts* is not a thing
    ; to say about it.
    procedure check_block(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindBlock
            this.check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(node)
        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.statements_of(node))
    }

    ; checkModuleDeclaration's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ITS FIRST STEP IS THE RECURSION, and that decides what the histogram
    ; says about a namespace: the body is checked BEFORE the module's own grammar,
    ; so a statement inside it that this port cannot answer is the FIRST report and
    ; therefore the tag (record_unported keeps the first). That is not a loss of
    ; resolution — it is the resolution moving one level down, which is what the
    ; block arm was ported for.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ORDER OF THE REPORTS IS NOT THE ORDER OF THE LINES, and the sort is
    ; what makes that safe: the body's diagnostics are found before the module's,
    ; while GetDiagnosticsForFile answers a list sorted by position. So a
    ; `declare module M { ; }` whose body reports TS1036 and whose header reports
    ; TS1035 comes out in text order either way.
    procedure check_module_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        let body AstNode.body_of(node)
        if body <> null
            this.check_source_element(body)

        let is_global_augmentation Binder.is_global_scope_augmentation(node)
        var in_ambient_context false
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            set in_ambient_context: true
        if is_global_augmentation
        {
            if in_ambient_context = false
                this.error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(node), DiagAugmentations_for_the_global_scope_should_have_declare_modifier_unless_they_appear_in_already_ambient_context)
        }

        ; ★ The two illegal-context messages are chosen by whether this is an
        ; AMBIENT EXTERNAL MODULE — `declare module "m"`, a quoted name — rather
        ; than by the ambient flag: `declare namespace N` is ambient and is still a
        ; namespace declaration.
        var context_code: int DiagA_namespace_declaration_is_only_allowed_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Binder.is_ambient_module(node)
            set context_code: DiagAn_ambient_module_declaration_is_only_allowed_at_the_top_level_in_a_file
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, context_code)
            return

        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if in_ambient_context = false
            {
                let name AstNode.name_of(node)
                if Checker.kind_or_unknown(name) = KindStringLiteral
                    this.grammar_error_on_node(name, DiagOnly_ambient_modules_can_use_quoted_names)
            }
        }

        ; ★ TWO TAGS, because the identifier branch stops one call earlier than the
        ; string one. `namespace N {}` is stopped by checkCollisionsForDeclarationName
        ; — and the TS1540 module-keyword advice sits BEHIND it, so `module N {}`
        ; cannot report it yet; `declare module "m"` skips that branch entirely and
        ; is stopped by checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations.
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.name_of(node)) = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("check-collisions-for-declaration-name", KindModuleDeclaration)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("check-exports-on-merged-declarations", KindModuleDeclaration)
    }

    ; checkImportDeclaration's prefix. The arm serves ImportDeclaration and
    ; JSImportDeclaration, as the reference's switch does — one `case` with two
    ; kinds — and the report carries the node's own kind so the histogram keeps
    ; them apart.
    ;
    ; ★ `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && node.Modifiers() != nil` is a
    ; short-circuit whose LEFT side is the call: the check always runs, and the
    ; extra report only follows when it found nothing and there is a modifier
    ; anyway. `An import declaration cannot have modifiers` is therefore about a
    ; LEGAL modifier in an illegal place (`export import x from "m"`), not about a
    ; malformed one.
    procedure check_import_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_import_declaration_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }
        this.record_unported("check-external-import-or-export-declaration", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration's prefix. It shares the import declaration's two
    ; illegal-context messages — the reference passes the same pair — and discards
    ; the modifier check's result, so there is no second modifier report here.
    procedure check_import_equals_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_import_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
        if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
        }
        ; ★ TWO TAGS for the two disjuncts of
        ; `IsInternalModuleImportEqualsDeclaration(node) || checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration(node)`:
        ; an INTERNAL `import A = B.C` answers the first and never asks the second,
        ; so what stops it is checkImportBinding, while `import A = require("m")`
        ; is stopped by the external check itself. One tag would put both behind a
        ; name that is wrong for one of them, and this column is the work list.
        if AstNode.import_equals_reference_kind(node) = KindExternalModuleReference
        {
            this.record_unported("check-external-import-or-export-declaration", KindImportEqualsDeclaration)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("check-import-binding", KindImportEqualsDeclaration)
    }

    ; checkExportDeclaration's prefix — and the ONE arm of this family that can run
    ; to the END.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `export {}` COMPLETES, and it is the only shape here that does. With no
    ; module specifier the `||` short-circuits, so checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration
    ; is never asked; with an EMPTY clause the specifier loop has nothing to visit;
    ; the three context bools then permit it at the top level of a file; and
    ; checkImportAttributes returns at its own first line because there is no
    ; `with { … }`. So the check reaches the bottom and the unit's C section becomes
    ; comparable — which is the only way the matched count can move in this slice,
    ; the same argument slice 49 made for why its count could NOT.
    procedure check_export_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        var code: int DiagAn_export_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_namespace_or_module
        if Parser.is_in_js_file(node)
            set code: DiagAn_export_declaration_can_only_be_used_at_the_top_level_of_a_module
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_export_declaration_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }
        this.check_grammar_export_declaration(node)

        if AstNode.module_specifier_of(node) <> null
        {
            this.record_unported("check-external-import-or-export-declaration", KindExportDeclaration)
            return
        }

        ; From here the module specifier is nil, which is the condition the three
        ; bools below and the `inAmbientNamespaceDeclaration` test all read.
        let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(node)
        var named false
        if clause <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(clause) <> KindNamespaceExport
                set named: true
        }
        if named = false
        {
            ; `export *` / `export * as ns` with no `from` — which the parser does
            ; not produce, so this is the reference's structure kept rather than a
            ; live path. resolveExternalModuleName is asked with a nil specifier
            ; and needs the module resolver either way.
            this.record_unported("resolve-external-module-name", KindExportDeclaration)
            return
        }

        let els AstNode.elements_of(clause)
        if AstNode.list_count(els) > 0
        {
            this.record_unported("check-export-specifier", KindExportDeclaration)
            return
        }

        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent)
        var in_ambient_external_module false
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
        {
            if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
                set in_ambient_external_module: true
        }
        var in_ambient_namespace_declaration false
        if in_ambient_external_module = false
        {
            if pk = KindModuleBlock
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    set in_ambient_namespace_declaration: true
            }
        }
        var permitted false
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set permitted: true
        if in_ambient_external_module
            set permitted: true
        if in_ambient_namespace_declaration
            set permitted: true
        if permitted = false
            this.error_on_node(node, DiagExport_declarations_are_not_permitted_in_a_namespace)

        this.check_import_attributes(node)
    }

    ; checkExportAssignment's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★ ONE PATH OF IT RETURNS, and it is unreachable today for the reason the
    ; family's header gives: the container walk finds a ModuleDeclaration only for
    ; an `export = x` inside a namespace BODY, and a body's statements are reached
    ; through this slice's own block arm — so the path exists, and the corpus is
    ; what says whether anything takes it.
    ;
    ; ★ `ast.IsExportAssignment(node)` in the modifier condition is always true
    ; here: the reference's switch routes exactly one kind to this arm. Kept as the
    ; reference's structure would read, minus a test that cannot fail.
    procedure check_export_assignment(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        let is_export_equals AstNode.is_export_equals_of(node)
        var code: int DiagA_default_export_must_be_at_the_top_level_of_a_file_or_module_declaration
        if is_export_equals
            set code: DiagAn_export_assignment_must_be_at_the_top_level_of_a_file_or_module_declaration
        if this.check_grammar_module_element_context(node, code)
            return
        if this.should_check_erasable_syntax(node)
        {
            if is_export_equals
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagThis_syntax_is_not_allowed_when_erasableSyntaxOnly_is_enabled)
            }
        }

        var container AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) <> KindSourceFile
            set container: AstNode.parent_node_of(container)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(container) = false
            {
                if is_export_equals
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagAn_export_assignment_cannot_be_used_in_a_namespace)
                if is_export_equals = false
                    this.error_on_node(node, DiagA_default_export_can_only_be_used_in_an_ECMAScript_style_module)
                return
            }
        }

        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node) = false
        {
            if AstNode.modifiers_of(node) <> null
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_export_assignment_cannot_have_modifiers)
        }

        ; isIllegalExportDefaultInCJS. VerbatimModuleSyntax stands in front of the
        ; whole conjunction and is unset, so the term is false without asking.
        ; Behind the option rather than omitted, and under the tag slice 49 gave the
        ; same missing machinery. ★What used to stand here added *and it reaches
        ; program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, which the stub program does not have*;
        ; slice 54 measured that and it is false — the oracle's program HAS the
        ; method and answers ModuleKindNone. The option is the whole of the hole.
        if verbatim_module_syntax
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-verbatim-module-syntax", KindExportAssignment)
            return
        }

        ; ★ TWO TAGS again, and the second names the EXPRESSION's kind rather than
        ; the statement's — check_expression_statement's argument: this column is
        ; the work list, and `ExportAssignment` is a row nobody can act on.
        let e AstNode.expression_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(e) = KindIdentifier
        {
            this.record_unported("resolve-entity-name", KindExportAssignment)
            return
        }
        this.record_unported("check-expression", Checker.kind_or_unknown(e))
    }

    ; checkImportAttributes' GUARD, which is the whole of it for a declaration with
    ; no `with { … }` clause — and that is every declaration in the corpus but a
    ; handful. Behind the guard stands getGlobalImportAttributesTypeChecked, i.e.
    ; the global type table and the assignability relation, so the rest is a
    ; dimension rather than a step.
    procedure check_import_attributes(this, declaration: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.import_attributes_of(declaration) = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-import-attributes", AstNode.kind_of(declaration))
    }

    ; ── slice 51: the class declaration ─────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SLICE IS THE HEAD checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration AND THE ONE ARM
    ; IT PREFIXES, and the line it stops at is the same KIND of line slice 50's
    ; was — a call into a CHECK the port does not have — but it takes three
    ; functions to get there rather than one, because two of them turn out to be
    ; guards. checkClassDeclaration's own prefix is a legacy-decorator report and
    ; the missing-name test; checkClassLikeDeclaration's first line is the head;
    ; and checkDecorators, which follows it, RETURNS at its own first line for
    ; every class that carries no decorator — which is nearly all of them. So the
    ; report lands on checkCollisionsForDeclarationName, a row this column already
    ; has for the module and enum arms.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT IT COST IS NOT IN THIS FILE: the head reports at spans only a
    ; NodeList's own range knows, so §3.5bq's side table had to become readable
    ; one phase later. See the class-like grammar checks below check_grammar_modifiers.

    ; checkDecorators' GUARD, and the guard is the whole of what is portable.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THREE-WAY `||` IS THE COMMON CASE. `!CanHaveDecorators(node) ||
    ; !HasDecorators(node) || !NodeCanBeDecorated(...)` returns for any node
    ; without a decorator, so this function is a no-op for a plain class and the
    ; check continues past it — which is the difference between reporting here and
    ; reporting one call earlier, and the reason it is ported at all.
    ;
    ; ★ Behind the guard stand checkExternalEmitHelpers (the emit-helper table, and
    ; a languageVersion this harness has no target for), markLinkedReferences and
    ; checkDecorator, which resolves a signature. None of them is a step; the tag
    ; names the function rather than one of them, because which one comes first
    ; depends on a compiler option that is not read anywhere yet.
    ;
    ; ★ The parent and grandparent are read null-safely, for the reason
    ; kind_or_unknown exists: upstream reads `node.Parent.Parent` straight out of
    ; the call and a nil Parent is a readable zero in Go.
    procedure check_decorators(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        if AstNode.can_have_decorators(node) = false
            return
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsDecorator) = false
            return
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var grandparent null as pointer[AstNode]
        if parent <> null
            set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        if this.node_can_be_decorated(node, parent, grandparent) = false
            return
        if Checker.find_first_decorator(node) = null
            return
        this.record_unported("check-decorators", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkClassLikeDeclaration's prefix — two calls and then the check that stops
    ; it. It is a function of its own rather than folded into the class arm because
    ; the reference has it that way and because checkClassExpression is its second
    ; caller, which is the next slice that reaches this file.
    procedure check_class_like_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_grammar_class_like_declaration(node)
        this.check_decorators(node)
        this.record_unported("check-collisions-for-declaration-name", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkClassDeclaration's prefix.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MISSING-NAME REPORT IS THE ONLY DIAGNOSTIC THIS ARM ADDS ON ITS OWN,
    ; and it is about `export default class {}` versus a bare `class {}` at
    ; statement position: the second has no name and no `default` modifier, and the
    ; span is the FIRST TOKEN rather than the error range, so the report is
    ; grammar_error_on_first_token.
    ;
    ; ★ `core.Find(node.ModifierNodes(), ast.IsDecorator)` is computed before the
    ; legacy test upstream and its value is only read inside it, so the order here
    ; is the reference's order with the dead computation left where a report can
    ; stand for it.
    procedure check_class_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        ; `c.legacyDecorators && firstDecorator != nil && core.Some(node.Members(),
        ; …)` — the class-decorator-with-a-static-private-member report. The option
        ; is unset under this harness (see check_grammar_modifiers' header), so the
        ; branch is dead; it is REPORTED rather than dropped for the reason the
        ; accessor-pair decorator check one screen down is reported — an options
        ; record arriving later turns it into a port instead of a silent wrong
        ; answer. What it needs besides the option is a class-MEMBERS accessor,
        ; which nothing in this port has a live reader for yet.
        if legacy_decorators
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-class-decorators-static-private", KindClassDeclaration)
            return
        }
        if AstNode.name_of(node) = null
        {
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsDefault) = false
                this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_class_declaration_without_the_default_modifier_must_have_a_name)
        }
        this.check_class_like_declaration(node)
        ; `c.checkSourceElements(node.Members())` and
        ; `c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(node)` are not reached: the call
        ; above always reports, so the MEMBERS of a class stay behind this arm
        ; rather than joining the histogram one level down the way a namespace
        ; body's statements did in slice 50.
    }

    ; ── slice 52: the function declaration ───────────────────────────────────

    ; checkTypeParameters, and the report is INSIDE the loop rather than in front
    ; of it, which is what makes a function without type parameters walk on.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE FORK IS THE SLICE'S SHAPE. `for i, node := range
    ; typeParameterDeclarations` is an EMPTY loop for every function that has no
    ; `<...>`, and the first call in its body — checkTypeParameter — resolves the
    ; parameter's constraint and default through getTypeFromTypeNode. So the head
    ; cannot carry one report for the whole arm: a generic function stops here and a
    ; plain one continues into its parameters, which is the histogram moving one
    ; level DOWN exactly as slice 50's block arm moved it onto a namespace body's
    ; statements. Behind the first call stand two more: the
    ; previously-declared-parameter walk of a default type, and the duplicate-name
    ; report, which needs the symbol a type parameter owns.
    procedure check_type_parameters(this, type_parameters: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]])
    {
        if type_parameters = null
            return
        if AstNode.list_count(type_parameters) = 0
            return
        this.record_unported("check-type-parameter", KindTypeParameter)
    }

    ; The six kinds whose checkSignatureDeclaration grammar arm is
    ; checkGrammarFunctionLikeDeclaration — the reference's own `case` list, kept
    ; as a list because the seventh (KindIndexSignature) has a DIFFERENT check and
    ; every other kind that reaches that switch has none.
    function kind_has_function_like_grammar_check(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return true
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        false
    }

    ; checkSignatureDeclaration, whole, with three of its steps written down rather
    ; than ported and each saying which kind of absence it is.
    procedure check_signature_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindIndexSignature
        {
            ; checkGrammarIndexSignature — the parameter-count, modifier, name,
            ; type-annotation and return-type checks of `[k: string]: T`. No arm of
            ; this slice can reach it (an index signature is a class or interface
            ; MEMBER, and neither container's members are walked yet), so the report
            ; stands for the whole of it.
            this.record_unported("check-grammar-index-signature", k)
        }
        else
        {
            if Checker.kind_has_function_like_grammar_check(k)
                this.check_grammar_function_like_declaration(node)
        }

        let function_flags b.get_function_flags(node)

        ; `if functionFlags&ast.FunctionFlagsInvalid == 0` guards two
        ; checkExternalEmitHelpers calls — the __await/__asyncGenerator helper for
        ; an async generator below ES2018, and __awaiter for an async function below
        ; ES2017. BOTH ARE DEAD, and for the reference's own default rather than for
        ; a gap in this port: languageVersion is GetEmitScriptTarget(), which
        ; answers ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 when Target is unset, so
        ; `languageVersion < ES2018` is false. That is the same read that makes the
        ; use-strict gate above always TAKEN — one unset option, two opposite
        ; consequences, which is why each is argued at its own site.

        this.check_type_parameters(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))

        ; checkUnmatchedJSDocParameters — UNREACHABLE, and it takes two conditions
        ; rather than one. getAllJSDocTags returns nil unless the node carries
        ; NodeFlagsHasJSDoc, and check_source_element_worker reports
        ; `check-jsdoc-comments` and RETURNS for any node that does — but that gate
        ; alone is NOT the argument, because the function walks UPWARDS through
        ; GetNextJSDocCommentLocation and an ancestor's tags would count. That walk
        ; goes to the parent only for eight kinds (PropertyAssignment,
        ; ExportAssignment, PropertyDeclaration, VariableDeclaration,
        ; SatisfiesExpression, ReturnStatement, VariableStatement,
        ; ExpressionStatement, and a VariableDeclarationList's first declaration),
        ; and a function declaration's parent is a SourceFile, a Block or a
        ; ModuleBlock — none of them. So the walk stops at the node itself and the
        ; gate decides. ★It expires for a DIFFERENT arm the moment one whose parent
        ; can be a VariableStatement lands: a function EXPRESSION.

        this.check_source_elements(AstNode.parameters_of(node))

        let return_type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if return_type_node <> null
            this.check_source_element(return_type_node)

        ; `if c.noImplicitAny && returnTypeNode == nil` — a switch with arms for
        ; ConstructSignature and CallSignature only, so no kind this slice reaches
        ; can report there. ★And noImplicitAny is TRUE under this harness, not
        ; false: it is a strict-FAMILY option, so an unset value falls through to
        ; `Strict != TSFalse` (strict_null_checks' header has the three readings of
        ; "unset" this slice met). So the two arms are live code behind a live
        ; option, and what the slice that ports either of them owes is the arm, not
        ; the option.

        if return_type_node <> null
        {
            ; Two type checks on the annotation, and the flag tests are
            ; EQUALITY tests against a composite (FunctionFlags.scaly's header says
            ; why): a generator that is not invalid, and an async function that is
            ; not a generator. Both resolve the annotation through
            ; getTypeFromTypeNode, so both report — separately, because the second
            ; is also the whole of checkAsyncFunctionReturnType and the two arrive
            ; in different slices.
            if (function_flags & (FunctionFlagsInvalid | FunctionFlagsGenerator)) = FunctionFlagsGenerator
                this.record_unported("check-generator-return-type", k)
            else
            {
                if (function_flags & FunctionFlagsAsyncGenerator) = FunctionFlagsAsync
                    this.record_unported("check-async-function-return-type", k)
            }
        }

        ; `if !ast.IsIndexSignatureDeclaration(node) { c.registerForUnusedIdentifiersCheck(node) }`
        ; — observable only through checkUnusedIdentifiers, which checkUnused gates
        ; off under this harness. checkBlock carries the same line for the same
        ; reason.
    }

    ; checkFunctionOrMethodDeclaration's prefix — two calls, and the third thing it
    ; does is where this slice stops.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SYMBOL CHECK RUNS FOR EVERY FUNCTION DECLARATION, NOT FOR SOME, and
    ; that is why the report is unconditional rather than behind the guard the
    ; reference writes. `c.hasBindableName(node)` is `!ast.HasDynamicName(node) ||
    ; c.hasLateBindableName(node)`, and HasDynamicName is `name != nil &&
    ; IsDynamicName(name)` — a computed or late-bound name. A function
    ; declaration's name is an Identifier or absent, so the left disjunct is TRUE
    ; on every one of them and checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol is reached. Writing
    ; the guard and reporting inside it would suggest a shape the corpus does not
    ; contain.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT STANDS BEHIND IT IS THE BODY, AND THAT IS THE SUCCESSOR. The
    ; reference's next line is `c.checkSourceElement(node.Body())` — a Block, an arm
    ; this port HAS since slice 50 — so the whole statement population of every
    ; function in the corpus is one report away. It is deliberately not taken here:
    ; the rule this column is built on (slice 49) is that the ported prefix runs up
    ; to the first thing the slice does not implement and the report NAMES that
    ; thing, and reaching past checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol to pick up a later
    ; step would make the tag a statement about what was convenient. The bill for
    ; the successor is that one function: the symbol's declaration list walked for a
    ; duplicate implementation, an overload without one, and the modifier/abstract
    ; agreement across declarations.
    ;
    ; ★ Between the two lies `functionFlags := ast.GetFunctionFlags(node)`, whose
    ; readers are all behind the symbol check, and the computed-property-name arm —
    ; `if node.Name() != nil && ast.IsComputedPropertyName(node.Name())` — which
    ; belongs to this function's OTHER caller: a method's name can be `[Symbol.x]`,
    ; a function declaration's cannot.
    procedure check_function_or_method_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_decorators(node)
        this.check_signature_declaration(node)
        this.record_unported("check-function-or-constructor-symbol", AstNode.kind_of(node))
    }

    ; checkFunctionDeclaration, whole — one call plus two the call above stops.
    ;
    ; ★ checkGrammarForGenerator (the `*` on an ambient or overload declaration) and
    ; checkCollisionsForDeclarationName (a row this column already has from the
    ; class, enum and module arms) both follow the line above, so neither is
    ; reached; they are named here rather than left out so that the slice which
    ; retires the report above finds them already written down.
    procedure check_function_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode])
    {
        this.check_function_or_method_declaration(node)
    }

    ; checkDeferredNodes. The reference walks sourceFileLinks.deferredNodes — the
    ; function and class BODIES that checkExpression parks so that a recursive
    ; reference does not force an `any`. Nothing ported defers anything, so the
    ; set is empty by construction; the call stands where the reference has it,
    ; because the arm that first defers a body must find the drain already in
    ; place rather than add it silently.
    procedure check_deferred_nodes(this)
    {
    }

    ; checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements, over the list checkVariableLikeDeclaration
    ; fills. Empty by construction here for the same reason: its only writer is a
    ; binding-element arm that reports.
    procedure check_unused_renamed_binding_elements(this)
    {
    }

    ; produceDeferredDiagnostics, over deferredDiagnosticCallbacks. Empty for the
    ; same reason again — addDeferredDiagnostic's callers are all inside arms that
    ; report — and kept for the same reason: a diagnostic that is deferred and
    ; never produced is invisible, so the drain belongs in place from the start.
    procedure produce_deferred_diagnostics(this)
    {
    }

    ; ── the grammar checks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.hasParseDiagnostics(sourceFile), whole: `len(sourceFile.Diagnostics()) > 0`.
    ; ★ It is the PARSE list and not the JS-SYNTAX one: sf.Diagnostics() does not
    ; include the second list (tscaly_ast.scaly's dump has them as separate
    ; sections for exactly that reason), so a file whose only complaint is a
    ; TS8000-series JS-syntax error still gets its grammar checks.
    function has_parse_diagnostics(this) returns bool
        AstNode.diagnostic_count(file) > 0

    ; grammarErrorOnFirstToken, whole. The span is the FIRST TOKEN at the node's
    ; own pos — not GetErrorRangeForNode, so no declaration name and no
    ; trivia-skip-then-name is involved — which is the same distinction
    ; binder.error_on_first_token draws one phase earlier.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PARSE-DIAGNOSTIC GUARD IS NOT A TIDINESS PASS. A file that failed to
    ; parse has a tree the grammar checks would read as a stream of violations, so
    ; the reference suppresses ALL of them and answers false — and the caller reads
    ; that false as *no grammar error here*, which is how `checkVariableStatement`'s
    ; `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && …` chain keeps running. A port that
    ; reported anyway would add diagnostics on exactly the units the parser
    ; yardstick already calls broken.
    function grammar_error_on_first_token(this, n: pointer[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        b.range_of_token_at_position(AstNode.pos_of(n), &start, &stop)
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
        true
    }

    ; grammarErrorAtPos, whole — the third of the three reporters, and the one that
    ; reports at a span NO NODE has.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT TAKES NO NODE. Upstream's first parameter is `nodeForSourceFile` and
    ; its only use is `ast.GetSourceFileOfNode(node)`: this checker has exactly one
    ; file and reads it from its own field, so a parameter for it would be a second
    ; thing to keep in step (the same reasoning `has_parse_diagnostics` above
    ; follows, where the reference threads the file and this does not).
    ;
    ; ★ `length` rather than an end, because that is the reference's shape and the
    ; two callers below both pass a literal — `len(",")` and a computed `end-start`.
    function grammar_error_at_pos(this, start: int, length: int, code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, start + length, code)
        true
    }

    ; ── three null-safe readers the grammar checks need ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ The reference reads `node.Parent.Kind` and `node.Parent.Flags` straight
    ; out of checkGrammarModifiers, six times, because in Go a nil Parent field is
    ; a readable zero — `AstNode.kind_of` here DEREFERENCES. The assumption behind
    ; the reference's spelling is true for every node these checks reach (a bound
    ; declaration has a parent), but an assumption that is true today and crashes
    ; when it stops being true is exactly the shape this port keeps paying for, so
    ; the question is asked instead of assumed. KindUnknown is 0 and matches no
    ; kind test, which is what makes the substitution exact.
    function kind_or_unknown(n: pointer[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        AstNode.kind_of(n)
    }

    function flags_or_none(n: pointer[AstNode]) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return NodeFlagsNone
        AstNode.flags_of(n)
    }

    function is_ambient_module_or_null(n: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        Binder.is_ambient_module(n)
    }

    ; grammarErrorOnNode, whole:
    ;
    ;   if !c.hasParseDiagnostics(sourceFile) { c.error(node, message); return true }
    ;   return false
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS NOT grammar_error_on_first_token WITH ANOTHER SPAN. `c.error` goes
    ; through NewDiagnosticForNode, i.e. scanner.GetErrorRangeForNode — the same
    ; function the BINDER's error_on_node uses — so a declaration reports at its
    ; NAME and everything else at its own trivia-skipped start. The distinction is
    ; the one binder.scaly draws between its two reporters, one phase earlier, and
    ; the two sit ten lines apart there for the same reason they do here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE SPAN MACHINERY IS THE BINDER'S, WHICH IS WHERE SLICE 45's NOTE
    ; SAID THIS BILL WOULD ARRIVE. error_range_for_node's eight deferred arms carry
    ; the line *THE READER THAT EARNS THEM IS THE CHECKER … the checker dimension's
    ; first bill*; this is the caller it named. It answers false on one of those
    ; arms, and then the honest move is to report UNPORTED rather than to invent a
    ; span: a wrong span is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, and the
    ; fifth yardstick compares spans. ★For the kinds this slice reaches the arm
    ; cannot fire — a modifier is a keyword token and the five node kinds are not
    ; on the list — so the report is a guard against the next slice rather than a
    ; live path, which is exactly why it is written now.
    function grammar_error_on_node(this, n: pointer[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        if b.error_range_for_node(n, &start, &stop) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("grammar-error-range", AstNode.kind_of(n))
            return false
        }
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
        true
    }

    ; c.error, whole: `NewDiagnosticForNode(location, message)` then addDiagnostic.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS grammar_error_on_node WITHOUT THE PARSE GUARD, and that one missing
    ; line is the whole difference between the two reporters. A grammar check is
    ; suppressed on a file that failed to parse — its tree would read as a stream
    ; of violations — while a SEMANTIC error is not, because the reference reports
    ; those regardless. So the two cannot share an implementation, and the span
    ; machinery they do share is the binder's error_range_for_node.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference returns the *Diagnostic, for the two callers that attach
    ; related information to it (addTypeOnlyDeclarationRelatedInfo). Neither is in
    ; this slice's prefix, and the yardstick compares no related information —
    ; tscaly_types dumps `C pos end code` — so this answers nothing. The first
    ; caller that needs the value is the one that owes the return type.
    procedure error_on_node(this, n: pointer[AstNode], code: int)
    {
        var start 0
        var stop 0
        if b.error_range_for_node(n, &start, &stop) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("error-range", AstNode.kind_of(n))
            return
        }
        diagnostics.add_direct(start, stop, code)
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarModuleElementContext — slice 50's head ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ SIX LINES, AND IT IS THE HEAD OF SIX ARMS. Slice 49 could take the five
    ; kinds whose arm BEGINS with checkGrammarModifiers and had to leave the
    ; import/export/module family, because this function stands in front of that
    ; call there and may RETURN — porting the modifier check into those arms
    ; without it would have run it where the reference does not.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE RESULT IS NOT THE REPORT'S RESULT, and the distinction is load-bearing
    ; in exactly the case grammar_error_on_first_token exists for. Upstream answers
    ; `!isInAppropriateContext`, so on a file that failed to parse the report is
    ; suppressed and this still answers TRUE — the caller bails out either way,
    ; which is what its comment asks for (*just bail out to avoid cascading
    ; errors*). Answering the report's bool instead would run the whole arm on a
    ; broken tree.
    ;
    ; ★ The three appropriate parents are read null-safely (kind_or_unknown) for
    ; the reason its own note gives: Go reads a nil Parent as a zero and
    ; AstNode.kind_of dereferences.
    function check_grammar_module_element_context(this, node: pointer[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
    {
        let pk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
        var in_appropriate_context false
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if pk = KindModuleDeclaration
            set in_appropriate_context: true
        if in_appropriate_context
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, code)
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarExportDeclaration, whole. `export type { T }` may not repeat the
    ; `type` on a specifier.
    function check_grammar_export_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.is_type_only_of(node) = false
            return false
        let clause AstNode.export_clause_of(node)
        if clause = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(clause) <> KindNamedExports
            return false
        this.check_grammar_type_only_named_imports_or_exports(clause)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeOnlyNamedImportsOrExports. The reference's two branches read
    ; the SAME field (`specifier.IsTypeOnly()`) and differ only in the message, so
    ; the port keeps one read and picks the code — which is the §3.5n test applied
    ; to a branch rather than to a record.
    ;
    ; ★ It reports on the FIRST type-only specifier and returns, so a clause with
    ; two of them answers one diagnostic. That is the reference's `return` inside
    ; the loop and not a dedup.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE IMPORT SIDE IS PORTED AND UNREACHABLE FROM THIS SLICE, which is worth
    ; saying so it does not read as measured: its only caller is
    ; checkGrammarImportClause, which sits behind checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration
    ; in checkImportDeclaration — the call this slice reports. It goes live with
    ; that one. Ported anyway, because this is ONE function and splitting it by
    ; caller would be a partial port of a function rather than a smaller slice.
    function check_grammar_type_only_named_imports_or_exports(this, named_bindings: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let els AstNode.elements_of(named_bindings)
        let n AstNode.list_count(els)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let specifier AstNode.child_in_list(els, i)
            var code: int DiagThe_type_modifier_cannot_be_used_on_a_named_export_when_export_type_is_used_on_its_export_statement
            if AstNode.kind_of(specifier) = KindImportSpecifier
                set code: DiagThe_type_modifier_cannot_be_used_on_a_named_import_when_import_type_is_used_on_its_import_statement
            if AstNode.is_type_only_of(specifier)
                return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(specifier, code)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; shouldCheckErasableSyntax, whole. ★ ErasableSyntaxOnly is unset under this
    ; harness, so this is false everywhere and its two readers carry LIVE code
    ; behind it rather than a report — see the field's note for why that is the
    ; right side of the line.
    function should_check_erasable_syntax(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if erasable_syntax_only = false
            return false
        Parser.is_in_js_file(node) = false
    }

    ; ── the class-like grammar checks — slice 51's head ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HEAD IS TWO LINES AND THE BILL IS A SIDE TABLE. checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration
    ; is `checkGrammarClassDeclarationHeritageClauses || checkGrammarTypeParameterList`,
    ; and BOTH halves report at a span that only a NodeList's own TextRange knows:
    ; `HasTrailingComma()` is `last.End() < list.End()` and an EMPTY bracketed list
    ; reports from `list.Pos()-len("<")` to `SkipTrivia(text, list.End())+len(">")`.
    ; This port's lists are plain Arrays, so §3.5bq's side table — built in slice 24
    ; for three JS-syntax arms of the PARSER — is the mechanism these checks need,
    ; and a Checker holds no Parser. It travels on the SourceFile now, beside the
    ; two diagnostic lists (ast.scaly's ListRange has the argument).
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND IT IS THE FIRST TIME ANY INSTRUMENT IN THIS PACKAGE CAN SEE A LIST'S
    ; RANGE. No yardstick dumps one — §3.5bq says so and that is exactly why the
    ; table was built with its own measurement rather than a test — so until this
    ; slice a wrong row and a right row compared equal everywhere. Three of the
    ; reports below are the first readers that can tell them apart.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PARSE GUARD IS HOISTED IN FRONT OF EVERY RANGE LOOKUP, and that is an
    ; equivalence rather than a shortcut. Each of these checks computes a span and
    ; then hands it to grammar_error_at_pos, which answers false on a file that
    ; failed to parse; computing the span has no side effects, so testing the guard
    ; first gives the same answer for every input. It buys two things. A MISSING
    ; list — parse_bracketed_list's sentinel, handed out when the opening bracket
    ; was absent — carries no row, and it cannot arrive here: the only way to get
    ; one is a failed parse_expected, which reported. And a missing row is then
    ; reported only where it could have changed an answer.
    function list_range_of(this, list: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]], out_pos: pointer[int], out_end: pointer[int]) returns bool
        AstNode.list_range_of(file, list, out_pos, out_end)

    ; checkGrammarForDisallowedTrailingComma, whole. `list.HasTrailingComma()` is
    ; DERIVED upstream too — `len(Nodes) > 0 && last.End() < list.End()` — so the
    ; row is the only thing this needs that the elements do not already carry.
    ;
    ; ★ The message is a parameter here as it is upstream, because the two callers
    ; pass different ones; the reference's second argument (`list.Nodes[0]`) is the
    ; source-file lookup grammar_error_at_pos does not take.
    function check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(this, list: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]], code: int) returns bool
    {
        let n AstNode.list_count(list)
        if n = 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(list, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        if AstNode.end_of(AstNode.child_in_list(list, n - 1)) >= lend
            return false
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(lend - 1, 1, code)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeParameterList, whole. It fires on a list that EXISTS and is
    ; EMPTY — `class C<> {}`, which parses cleanly, which is the whole reason the
    ; grammar check exists rather than a parse diagnostic.
    ;
    ; ★ `- len("<")` and `+ len(">")` are the reference's own spelling of the two
    ; brackets the list's range sits inside; the trivia skip in between is why the
    ; end cannot be taken from the range directly.
    function check_grammar_type_parameter_list(this, type_parameters: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]) returns bool
    {
        if type_parameters = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(type_parameters) <> 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(type_parameters, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        let start lpos - 1
        let stop b.skip_trivia_at(lend) + 1
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(start, stop - start, DiagType_parameter_list_cannot_be_empty)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForAtLeastOneTypeArgument — the same shape one message along.
    ; Upstream asks GetSourceFileOfNode(node) for the text and the report's file;
    ; this checker has one file and the parameter goes with it.
    function check_grammar_for_at_least_one_type_argument(this, type_arguments: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]) returns bool
    {
        if type_arguments = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(type_arguments) <> 0
            return false
        if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
            return false
        var lpos 0
        var lend 0
        if this.list_range_of(type_arguments, &lpos, &lend) = false
        {
            this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
            return false
        }
        let start lpos - 1
        let stop b.skip_trivia_at(lend) + 1
        this.grammar_error_at_pos(start, stop - start, DiagType_argument_list_cannot_be_empty)
    }

    ; checkGrammarTypeArguments, whole — the `||` of the two above, in that order.
    function check_grammar_type_arguments(this, type_arguments: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(type_arguments, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true
        this.check_grammar_for_at_least_one_type_argument(type_arguments)
    }

    ; checkGrammarExpressionWithTypeArguments, whole. Its parameter is upstream's
    ; `Union[ExpressionWithTypeArguments, TypeQuery]`, and type_arguments_of answers
    ; both — which is one of the nine arms slice 30 widened it to (§3.5br).
    function check_grammar_expression_with_type_arguments(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
        {
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.expression_of(node)) = KindImportKeyword
            {
                if AstNode.type_arguments_of(node) <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagThis_use_of_import_is_invalid_import_calls_can_be_written_but_they_must_have_parentheses_and_cannot_have_type_arguments)
            }
        }
        this.check_grammar_type_arguments(AstNode.type_arguments_of(node))
    }

    ; checkGrammarHeritageClause, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENT IS DROPPED, on checkGrammarModifiers' argument one
    ; screen up: `{0} list cannot be empty` is filled from
    ; scanner.TokenToString(node.Token), a message's CODE does not depend on its
    ; arguments, and the dump is `C pos end code`. TokenToString is therefore still
    ; unported, and the bill for it is named where that note names it.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference reaches `types.Nodes` unguarded after the empty test, so a nil
    ; types would panic there; the guard here is not a divergence but the question
    ; asked instead of assumed — parse_heritage_clause always builds the list, which
    ; is why upstream can write it that way.
    function check_grammar_heritage_clause(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let types AstNode.heritage_clause_types_of(node)
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(types, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true
        if types = null
            return false
        if AstNode.list_count(types) = 0
        {
            if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
                return false
            var lpos 0
            var lend 0
            if this.list_range_of(types, &lpos, &lend) = false
            {
                this.record_unported("list-range", 0)
                return false
            }
            ; grammarErrorAtPos(node, types.Pos(), 0, …) — a ZERO-WIDTH span at the
            ; list's own start, with the reference's own `why not error on the
            ; token?` left standing beside it.
            return this.grammar_error_at_pos(lpos, 0, DiagX_0_list_cannot_be_empty)
        }
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(types)
        while i < n
        {
            if this.check_grammar_expression_with_type_arguments(AstNode.child_in_list(types, i))
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarClassDeclarationHeritageClauses, whole but one loop.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE JSDOC `@augments` WALK IS UNREACHABLE HERE, BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER
    ; THAN BY THE CORPUS. Upstream runs `for _, j := range node.EagerJSDoc(file)`
    ; over the extends clause's single type, and EagerJSDoc answers nil unless
    ; NodeFlagsHasJSDoc is set — while check_source_element_worker reports
    ; `check-jsdoc-comments` and RETURNS for any node carrying that flag, one call
    ; before this arm runs. So the loop's body cannot execute, and it is omitted
    ; with its condition written down rather than ported as dead code: what it
    ; needs beyond the walk is getIdentifierFromEntityNameExpression, the tag's
    ; ClassName and a three-argument message. ★It expires the moment the JSDoc
    ; comment check is ported — that gate is the whole of the argument.
    ;
    ; ★★ UPSTREAM PANICS ON A HERITAGE TOKEN THAT IS NEITHER `extends` NOR
    ; `implements`, and the else branch here simply takes the implements reading.
    ; parse_heritage_clause is reached only on one of those two keywords and writes
    ; the token it saw, so the third case does not exist on either side; a trap
    ; would be a claim about a shape the parser cannot build.
    ;
    ; ★ `!c.checkGrammarModifiers(node) && classLikeData.HeritageClauses != nil` is
    ; a short-circuit whose LEFT side is the call, so the modifier check always
    ; runs — the same shape checkImportDeclaration's has, and the reason a class
    ; with no heritage clause at all still reports its modifier errors.
    function check_grammar_class_declaration_heritage_clauses(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var seen_extends_clause false
        var seen_implements_clause false
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
            return false
        let clauses AstNode.class_heritage_clauses_of(node)
        if clauses = null
            return false
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(clauses)
        while i < n
        {
            let clause AstNode.child_in_list(clauses, i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.heritage_clause_token_of(clause) = KindExtendsKeyword
            {
                if seen_extends_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_extends_clause_already_seen)
                if seen_implements_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_extends_clause_must_precede_implements_clause)
                let types AstNode.heritage_clause_types_of(clause)
                if AstNode.list_count(types) > 1
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(AstNode.child_in_list(types, 1), DiagClasses_can_only_extend_a_single_class)
                set seen_extends_clause: true
            }
            else
            {
                if seen_implements_clause
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(clause, DiagX_implements_clause_already_seen)
                set seen_implements_clause: true
            }
            ; The reference DISCARDS this result — it is a statement of its own,
            ; after the extends/implements bookkeeping — so a report inside one
            ; clause does not stop the walk over the rest.
            this.check_grammar_heritage_clause(clause)
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration, whole: two calls and an `||`.
    function check_grammar_class_like_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_class_declaration_heritage_clauses(node)
            return true
        this.check_grammar_type_parameter_list(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
    }

    ; ── slice 52: the function-like grammar checks ───────────────────────────

    ; checkGrammarParameterList, whole. Four of its five reports are about the
    ; REST parameter and the fifth is the optional/required order; every span is a
    ; node this port already has, so nothing here needs the side table the type
    ; parameter list above needs.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE TRAILING-COMMA CALL IS THE ONLY ONE WHOSE RESULT IS DISCARDED, and
    ; that is the reference's own shape: a rest parameter with a trailing comma
    ; reports TS1013 and the walk CONTINUES into the question-mark and initializer
    ; tests of the same parameter, so a `...a?,` yields two diagnostics. Reading
    ; that call as a `return` would drop the second.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AMBIENT GUARD IS ON THE PARAMETER, NOT ON THE FILE. `parameter.Flags
    ; & NodeFlagsAmbient == 0` — the flag is propagated down from the `declare`, so
    ; in a .d.ts every parameter carries it and the trailing comma is allowed there.
    ; A file-level test would be the same answer for the corpus's declaration files
    ; and a wrong one for a `declare function` in a .ts.
    ;
    ; ★ isOptionalDeclaration is ast.HasQuestionToken, which is
    ; `IsQuestionToken(node.QuestionToken())` — a null test plus a kind test, and
    ; question_token_of already answers null for anything that is not one (its
    ; postfix arm checks the kind). So the second arm's own `parameter.QuestionToken
    ; != nil` is not a second question: it is the same one, re-asked because
    ; upstream's helper took the node and its body wants the token.
    function check_grammar_parameter_list(this, parameters: pointer[Array[pointer[AstNode]]]) returns bool
    {
        if parameters = null
            return false
        var seen_optional_parameter false
        let parameter_count AstNode.list_count(parameters)
        var i 0
        while i < parameter_count
        {
            let parameter AstNode.child_in_list(parameters, i)
            let index i
            set i: i + 1
            let dot_dot_dot AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(parameter)
            let question AstNode.question_token_of(parameter)
            let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(parameter)
            if dot_dot_dot <> null
            {
                if index <> parameter_count - 1
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(dot_dot_dot, DiagA_rest_parameter_must_be_last_in_a_parameter_list)
                if (AstNode.flags_of(parameter) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
                    this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(parameters, DiagA_rest_parameter_or_binding_pattern_may_not_have_a_trailing_comma)
                if question <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(question, DiagA_rest_parameter_cannot_be_optional)
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagA_rest_parameter_cannot_have_an_initializer)
            }
            else
            {
                if question <> null
                {
                    set seen_optional_parameter: true
                    ; `parameter.QuestionToken.Flags & NodeFlagsReparsed == 0` — a
                    ; REPARSED `?` is the bracketed name of a JSDoc `@param [x]`
                    ; tag, which is not a syntax error to report at. ★It cannot be
                    ; reached from this slice's arm and is ported anyway: a `@param`
                    ; tag puts NodeFlagsHasJSDoc on the function, and
                    ; check_source_element_worker returns on that flag one call
                    ; earlier. So no unit separates the guard from its absence —
                    ; §3.5cv's `@augments` shape, expiring with the same slice.
                    if (AstNode.flags_of(question) & NodeFlagsReparsed) = 0
                    {
                        if initializer <> null
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagParameter_cannot_have_question_mark_and_initializer)
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    if seen_optional_parameter
                    {
                        if initializer = null
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(AstNode.name_of(parameter), DiagA_required_parameter_cannot_follow_an_optional_parameter)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarArrowFunction's GUARD, and nothing behind it — the shape
    ; check_decorators has, and for the same reason: the guard is what makes the
    ; rest unreachable from this slice's one arm, so porting it is what lets the
    ; `||` chain above it run to completion.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS IS WHERE THE ECMA LINE MAP WOULD BE PAID FOR, AND IT IS NOT REACHED.
    ; Behind the guard stand two reports: the `.mts`/`.cts` reserved-syntax warning
    ; for a single type parameter without a constraint (which needs
    ; tspath.FileExtensionIsOneOf, and a list's HasTrailingComma, i.e. §3.5cv's side
    ; table), and *line terminator not permitted before arrow*, whose condition is
    ; GetECMALineOfPosition on both ends of the `=>` token — the LAST of
    ; error_range_for_node's eight deferred arms (§3.5bx). An arrow function is not
    ; a statement, so no arm of this slice can produce one; the report names the
    ; function rather than either half, because which of them comes first depends on
    ; the file's extension.
    function check_grammar_arrow_function(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindArrowFunction
            return false
        this.record_unported("check-grammar-arrow-function", KindArrowFunction)
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarForUseStrictSimpleParameterList, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE LANGUAGE-VERSION GATE IS LIVE, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE READING OF THE
    ; ONE `check-decorators` PARKED ON. That report stands behind an option the
    ; harness leaves UNSET and whose default is FALSE, so the branch is dead; this
    ; gate is `c.languageVersion >= core.ScriptTargetES2016`, and languageVersion is
    ; `compilerOptions.GetEmitScriptTarget()`, which answers
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard — ES2025, the HIGHEST standard target — when Target
    ; is unset. So an unset option makes this branch always TAKEN. Reading "the
    ; option is unset" as "the branch is dead" is a wrong answer half the time, and
    ; which half depends on the reference's own default. The same default is what
    ; kills the two checkExternalEmitHelpers calls in check_signature_declaration:
    ; `languageVersion < ES2018` is false at ES2025.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE RELATED INFO IS DROPPED AND THE DIAGNOSTICS ARE NOT. Upstream attaches
    ; `'use strict' directive used here` to each parameter report and
    ; `Non-simple parameter declared here` / `and here` to the directive's, through
    ; AddRelatedInfo — which ast.NewDiagnostic keeps ON the diagnostic rather than in
    ; the list, so no dump reaches it (the same argument check_grammar_modifiers'
    ; header makes about a message's ARGUMENTS). What the dump does see is one TS1346
    ; per non-simple parameter plus one TS1347 on the directive, and that is what
    ; this emits.
    ;
    ; ★ The filter is walked TWICE upstream (once to report on each parameter, once
    ; to attach the related info in index order) and once here, because the second
    ; walk produces nothing this port keeps. `core.Filter` therefore needs no list:
    ; the FIRST non-simple parameter decides whether anything is reported at all, so
    ; the loop counts and reports in one pass and the directive's report follows it.
    function check_grammar_for_use_strict_simple_parameter_list(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        ; c.languageVersion >= core.ScriptTargetES2016 — always true here, see the
        ; header. Written as the condition it is rather than dropped, because a
        ; harness that reads @target turns it back into a question.
        let body AstNode.body_of(node)
        if body = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(body) <> KindBlock
            return false
        let use_strict_directive b.find_use_strict_prologue(AstNode.statements_of(body))
        if use_strict_directive = null
            return false
        let parameters AstNode.parameters_of(node)
        if parameters = null
            return false
        var non_simple 0
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(parameters)
        while i < n
        {
            let parameter AstNode.child_in_list(parameters, i)
            set i: i + 1
            var is_non_simple false
            if AstNode.initializer_of(parameter) <> null
                set is_non_simple: true
            if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(parameter))
                set is_non_simple: true
            if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(parameter) <> null
                set is_non_simple: true
            if is_non_simple
            {
                set non_simple: non_simple + 1
                this.error_on_node(parameter, DiagThis_parameter_is_not_allowed_with_use_strict_directive)
            }
        }
        if non_simple = 0
            return false
        this.error_on_node(use_strict_directive, DiagX_use_strict_directive_cannot_be_used_with_non_simple_parameter_list)
        true
    }

    ; checkGrammarFunctionLikeDeclaration, whole: a five-term `||`, and the longest
    ; chain of grammar checks this port has run end to end (slice 51's
    ; checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration is two).
    ;
    ; ★ The last term's `ast.IsFunctionLikeDeclaration(node)` guard is what keeps a
    ; CallSignature or a FunctionType — which have no body — out of the use-strict
    ; check; the two kinds that would reach it with a body and are not function-like
    ; DECLARATIONS do not exist.
    function check_grammar_function_like_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.check_grammar_modifiers(node)
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_type_parameter_list(AstNode.type_parameters_of(node))
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_parameter_list(AstNode.parameters_of(node))
            return true
        if this.check_grammar_arrow_function(node)
            return true
        if AstNode.is_function_like_declaration(node) = false
            return false
        this.check_grammar_for_use_strict_simple_parameter_list(node)
    }

    ; ── slice 54: the variable DECLARATION's grammar ─────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarVariableDeclaration, whole and COMPLETE except for one term of
    ; checkAmbientInitializer's disjunction, named at its line. Five steps, and
    ; four of them are a `return` of a report, so the order below is the
    ; reference's rather than a convenience.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `__esModule` TERM IS REACHABLE, AND WHAT MAKES IT SO IS THE STUB
    ; PROGRAM'S ANSWER RATHER THAN THE REAL COMPILER'S — WHICH CORRECTS A CLAIM
    ; THIS FILE HAS CARRIED SINCE SLICE 49. Two places above say
    ; `program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile … the stub program does not have` / `does
    ; not answer`; it HAS it and it answers `core.ModuleKindNone`, i.e. **0**. The
    ; guard is `< core.ModuleKindSystem`, and System is **4**, so the term is
    ; TRUE for every file under this harness. Not merely reachable in principle —
    ; measured on the way in: `export var __esModule = 1` gets TS1216 out of the
    ; reference, and so does `export var [__esModule] = [1]`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE REAL COMPILER WOULD ANSWER THE OTHER WAY, which is why reading
    ; the reference's implementation instead of the ORACLE would have got this
    ; backwards. `GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile` there is `GetEmitModuleKind()` when
    ; the file's implied format is None — and with `Module` unset that is
    ; `GetEmitScriptTarget()` = ES2025 ⇒ **ModuleKindES2022 = 7**, and `7 < 4` is
    ; false. So this is a FIFTH reading of an unset option to set beside §3.5cw's
    ; three and §3.5cx's fourth, and it is a different KIND of reading: the value
    ; comes from the harness's stub, not from the option's default, and the two
    ; disagree. The rule that falls out of it: **an option's default is read from
    ; the constructor, but a PROGRAM's answer is read from the program the oracle
    ; builds** — tests/oracle/types.go, not internal/compiler/program.go.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE CONJUNCTION IS NOT REORDERED and does not need to be. Because the
    ; first term is a constant true here it is written as the comment it is, with
    ; the value that makes it true, and the two terms that can actually vary are
    ; asked. A port that had reported unported on the program layer would have
    ; buried a live report behind a hole that is not there.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DEFINITE-ASSIGNMENT TERM IS THE ONE REPORT OF THIS FUNCTION THAT IS
    ; NOT ON THE DECLARATION. `grammarErrorOnNode(node.ExclamationToken, …)` points
    ; at the token, and error_range_for_node has no arm for a token kind, so the
    ; span is the token's own trivia-skipped start — which is what the reference's
    ; TS1263 at the `!` of `let b!: number = 1` measures.
    ;
    ; ★ The three-way message choice collapses to three CODES and no argument, so
    ; §3.5ct's argument applies unchanged: the dump is `C pos end code`.
    function check_grammar_variable_declaration(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let node_flags Binder.combined_node_flags(node)
        let block_scope_kind node_flags & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        let name AstNode.name_of(node)
        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
        {
            if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_may_not_have_binding_patterns)
            if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_may_not_have_binding_patterns)
        }

        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        var grandparent null as pointer[AstNode]
        if parent <> null
            set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        let gk Checker.kind_or_unknown(grandparent)
        var in_for_in_or_of false
        if gk = KindForInStatement
            set in_for_in_or_of: true
        if gk = KindForOfStatement
            set in_for_in_or_of: true

        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        if in_for_in_or_of = false
        {
            if (node_flags & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            {
                ; The result is DISCARDED here, as it is upstream — the flow past
                ; this point does not depend on it — so an unported report inside
                ; does not stop the three checks below either.
                this.check_ambient_initializer(node)
            }
            else
            {
                if initializer = null
                {
                    if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(name)
                    {
                        if AstNode.is_binding_pattern(parent) = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_destructuring_declaration_must_have_an_initializer)
                    }
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_must_be_initialized)
                }
            }
        }

        let exclamation_token AstNode.exclamation_token_of(node)
        if exclamation_token <> null
        {
            var not_permitted false
            if gk <> KindVariableStatement
                set not_permitted: true
            if type_node = null
                set not_permitted: true
            if initializer <> null
                set not_permitted: true
            if (node_flags & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                set not_permitted: true
            if not_permitted
            {
                if initializer <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagDeclarations_with_initializers_cannot_also_have_definite_assignment_assertions)
                if type_node = null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagDeclarations_with_definite_assignment_assertions_must_also_have_type_annotations)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(exclamation_token, DiagA_definite_assignment_assertion_is_not_permitted_in_this_context)
            }
        }

        ; `c.program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile(sourceFile) < core.ModuleKindSystem`.
        ; The oracle's program answers ModuleKindNone = 0 and System is 4, so the
        ; term is a constant TRUE under this harness — see the header above, and
        ; note that it is the ORACLE's answer and not the real compiler's.
        if grandparent <> null
        {
            if (AstNode.flags_of(grandparent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            {
                if b.has_syntactic_modifier(grandparent, ModifierFlagsExport)
                {
                    this.check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(name)
                }
            }
        }

        if block_scope_kind = 0
            return false
        this.check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(name)
    }

    ; checkGrammarForEsModuleMarkerInBindingName, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT RETURNS OUT OF THE LOOP ON THE FIRST ELEMENT THAT HAS A NAME, which
    ; is upstream's `return` inside the `for` and not a `continue` — so a pattern
    ; whose first named element is innocent hides a `__esModule` behind it. That is
    ; transcribed rather than tidied: it is the reference's answer, and the
    ; measurement that fixed the shape of this function is the same probe as the
    ; header's — `export var { __esModule: q } = …` gets NO report, because a
    ; binding element's NAME is `q` and the property name is not asked.
    function check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(this, name: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if name = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "__esModule", 10)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node_skipped_on_no_emit(name, DiagIdentifier_expected_esModule_is_reserved_as_an_exported_marker_when_transforming_ECMAScript_modules)
            return false
        }
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
        while i < n
        {
            let element AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
            set i: i + 1
            let en AstNode.name_of(element)
            if en <> null
                return this.check_grammar_for_es_module_marker_in_binding_name(en)
        }
        false
    }

    ; grammarErrorOnNodeSkippedOnNoEmit, whole. It is grammar_error_on_node plus
    ; `d.SetSkippedOnNoEmit()`, a flag the emitter reads to drop the diagnostic
    ; when nothing is emitted.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FLAG IS NOT CARRIED, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A SHRUG.
    ; The oracle prints every line `c.GetDiagnostics` answers, and GetDiagnostics
    ; does not filter on it — so a skipped-on-no-emit diagnostic is in the C
    ; section like any other, which is how the reference's TS1216 was seen at all.
    ; The flag becomes observable on the day something in this repository EMITS,
    ; and until then a field for it would be a field nothing reads.
    function grammar_error_on_node_skipped_on_no_emit(this, n: pointer[AstNode], code: int) returns bool
        this.grammar_error_on_node(n, code)

    ; checkGrammarNameInLetOrConstDeclarations, whole.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT ALWAYS ANSWERS FALSE FOR A PATTERN and true only for a bare `let`, and
    ; the asymmetry is upstream's: the recursion's result is DISCARDED, so a
    ; pattern reports once per offending element and still answers false to its
    ; caller. That is the opposite discipline to the `__esModule` walk above, four
    ; lines away in the reference, and the two are deliberately not unified.
    ;
    ; ★ The text test is against the SPELLING `let`, not against the keyword's
    ; token kind: this position is an identifier by the time the parser is done
    ; with it (`let let = 1` parses), so what is left is a name comparison.
    function check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(this, name: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if name = null
            return false
        if AstNode.kind_of(name) = KindIdentifier
        {
            if Checker.identifier_text_equals(name, "let", 3)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(name, DiagX_let_is_not_allowed_to_be_used_as_a_name_in_let_or_const_declarations)
            return false
        }
        let elements AstNode.elements_of(name)
        var i 0
        let n AstNode.list_count(elements)
        while i < n
        {
            let element AstNode.child_in_list(elements, i)
            set i: i + 1
            let en AstNode.name_of(element)
            if en <> null
                this.check_grammar_name_in_let_or_const_declarations(en)
        }
        false
    }

    ; An identifier's stored text against a FIXED ASCII spelling. The reference
    ; writes `name.Text() == "__esModule"`; this port keeps an identifier's text as
    ; (buffer, length) rather than as a String, so the compare is a length test and
    ; a byte loop.
    ;
    ; ★ BYTES rather than runes, and it is exact: both spellings are ASCII, and the
    ; stored text is already the ESCAPE-DECODED value, so `let`
    ; arrives here as `let` and compares equal — which is the reference's answer
    ; too, since it compares the same decoded text.
    function identifier_text_equals(n: pointer[AstNode], s: pointer[const_char], len: int) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.identifier_text_length_of(n) <> len
            return false
        let t AstNode.identifier_text_of(n)
        if t = null
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if (*(t + i) as int) <> ((*(s + i) as char) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; checkAmbientInitializer, whole for the ONE kind this slice reaches. The
    ; reference's switch has three arms (VariableDeclaration, PropertyDeclaration,
    ; PropertySignature) over two accessors each, and both accessors here answer for
    ; all three kinds already (`type_of`, `initializer_of`), so the switch collapses
    ; to nothing.
    ;
    ; ★ The `default: panic` goes with it, and the argument is about the CALLERS
    ; rather than the accessors: this function has exactly three call sites and each
    ; passes one of those three kinds, so the panic's population is empty. It is not
    ; that the accessors could not be handed a fourth kind — they would answer null
    ; and this would return false where the reference dies — it is that nothing
    ; hands them one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ONE TERM OF THE FIVE-WAY DISJUNCTION NEEDS THE TYPE MACHINERY, AND THE
    ; WHOLE CORPUS REACHES IT ONCE — IN ITS OWN FIXTURE.
    ; `isInitializerSimpleLiteralEnumReference` asks
    ; `checkExpressionCached(expr).flags & EnumLike` — but only for a PROPERTY
    ; ACCESS or an ELEMENT ACCESS, so for every other initializer shape the term is
    ; false without asking anything, and the disjunction is decided by the four
    ; kind tests around it. An ambient const initialized from a property access is
    ; rare enough that `is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference` is **1 unit
    ; of 17 863** on stage 2 and that unit is
    ; `checker_variable_ambient_initializer_enum_ref.d.ts` — the fixture IS the
    ; corpus here (§3.5cw's use-strict-prologue shape).
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS A REPORT AND NOT A `return false`, AND THE BATTERY IS WHY. Guessing
    ; *not an enum reference* agrees with the reference on every unit in reach —
    ; controls-slice54.sh's f13 makes that guess and comes back UNGATED — because no
    ; ambient const in the corpus is initialized from a real enum member. A guess
    ; that no instrument can distinguish from a port is the most expensive kind, so
    ; the term reports. Measured on the way in: `declare namespace P { const g =
    ; N.a }` gets TS1254 out of the reference, so the far side of the hole is real.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE HOLE IS ASKED IN THE PLACE THE ANSWER IS USED, not where the reference
    ; computes it. Upstream evaluates `isInvalidInitializer` eagerly and then throws
    ; it away in the else branch — where the report is TS1039 whatever the
    ; initializer is — so asking it only inside the const-and-untyped branch is the
    ; same answer with one fewer way to be wrong. What that costs is the
    ; checkExpressionCached CALL, which can report at its own sites; those are the
    ; expression dimension and are unported everywhere, so no line of ours moves.
    ;
    ; ★ `isConstOrReadonly` reads `readonly` through the COMBINED modifier flags, so
    ; a `readonly` the grammar has already refused on a variable statement still
    ; sets it — which is why the term is not simplified away for a kind that cannot
    ; carry the modifier legally.
    function check_ambient_initializer(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let initializer AstNode.initializer_of(node)
        if initializer = null
            return false
        let type_node AstNode.type_of(node)
        var is_const_or_readonly false
        if this.is_declaration_readonly(node)
            set is_const_or_readonly: true
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableDeclaration
        {
            if this.is_var_const_like(node)
                set is_const_or_readonly: true
        }
        if is_const_or_readonly = false
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagInitializers_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
        if type_node <> null
            return this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagInitializers_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)

        if Binder.is_string_or_numeric_literal_like(initializer)
            return false
        if Checker.is_initializer_signed_numeric_literal(initializer)
            return false
        let ik AstNode.kind_of(initializer)
        if ik = KindTrueKeyword
            return false
        if ik = KindFalseKeyword
            return false
        if Checker.is_initializer_bigint_literal_expression(initializer)
            return false
        ; isInitializerSimpleLiteralEnumReference, the one term this port cannot
        ; answer — see the header. It is false for every initializer that is not one
        ; of these two kinds, so the report is asked here and nowhere wider.
        if ik = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference", ik)
            return false
        }
        if ik = KindElementAccessExpression
        {
            this.record_unported("is-initializer-simple-literal-enum-reference", ik)
            return false
        }
        this.grammar_error_on_node(initializer, DiagA_const_initializer_in_an_ambient_context_must_be_a_string_or_numeric_literal_or_literal_enum_reference)
    }

    ; The second half of isInitializerStringOrNumberLiteralExpression: a PREFIX
    ; MINUS over a numeric literal. It is NOT ast.is_signed_numeric_literal, which
    ; admits a leading `+` as well — this one is minus-only, and a `+1` initializer
    ; in an ambient const IS refused by the reference.
    function is_initializer_signed_numeric_literal(expr: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) <> KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(expr) <> KindMinusToken
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(expr)) = KindNumericLiteral
    }

    ; isInitializerBigIntLiteralExpression, whole.
    function is_initializer_bigint_literal_expression(expr: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) = KindBigIntLiteral
            return true
        if AstNode.kind_of(expr) <> KindPrefixUnaryExpression
            return false
        if AstNode.prefix_unary_operator_kind(expr) <> KindMinusToken
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(AstNode.prefix_unary_operand_of(expr)) = KindBigIntLiteral
    }

    ; isDeclarationReadonly, whole. `GetCombinedModifierFlags` is the binder's
    ; has_combined_modifier — the modifier twin of combined_node_flags, and the two
    ; sit together there because upstream has them as ONE generic over the flag
    ; getter, which Scaly cannot spell (the note at combined_node_flags says so).
    function is_declaration_readonly(this, declaration: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if b.has_combined_modifier(declaration, ModifierFlagsReadonly) = false
            return false
        b.is_parameter_property_declaration(declaration) = false
    }

    ; isVarConstLike, whole.
    function is_var_const_like(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let block_scope_kind Binder.combined_node_flags(node) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
            return true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
            return true
        block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
    }

    ; ── slice 53: the variable declaration list's grammar ────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarVariableDeclarationList, whole. Five terms, and each is a
    ; `return` — so the first one that reports ends the function, which is what
    ; makes the order below the reference's rather than a convenience.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE EMPTY-LIST REPORT IS UNREACHABLE FROM THIS SLICE'S ONLY CALLER, AND
    ; SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF WRITING IT. The check fires on a list that EXISTS
    ; and has no elements, and the parser produces exactly one such list —
    ; `parse_variable_declaration_list`'s missing-list branch, for `for (let of X)`
    ; — which reaches this function only through checkForInOrForOfStatement, an arm
    ; this slice does not port. Every other empty declarations list is the
    ; delimited-list parser answering nothing, which means parse_expected reported,
    ; which means has_parse_diagnostics is true and grammar_error_at_pos answers
    ; false. So the term is transcribed, and the two other callers upstream
    ; (checkForStatement and checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement) are what will
    ; exercise it.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SPAN NEEDS THE LIST'S OWN RANGE, which is §3.5cv's side table again,
    ; and the missing list had no ROW until this slice. `createMissingList` is
    ; `parseEmptyNodeList` — `newNodeList(NewTextRange(p.nodePos(), p.nodePos()),
    ; nil)` — i.e. a zero-length range at the look-ahead position, and our missing
    ; branch built the Array without recording one. The row is added at the same
    ; point the reference computes it. Nothing in this port could see the
    ; difference before, which is why it had to be added by READING the reference
    ; rather than by a failing unit; the next slice is the first reader.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE AWAIT-USING TAIL IS REACHED BY THE CORPUS — 10 units of stage 2's
    ; 17 855 carry the tag, which is more than the fixture and less than a slice —
    ; AND IT ANSWERS **TRUE**, and the direction
    ; is the argument. checkGrammarAwaitOrAwaitUsing is a chapter of its own — the
    ; module kind, the implied node format of the file, IsInTopLevelContext,
    ; IsEffectiveExternalModule and a related-info attachment — and its result
    ; decides whether checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement runs at
    ; all. Answering FALSE would run a check the reference may skip and could put a
    ; TS1156 where the reference has none, which is the one thing diagcheck can
    ; fail on. Answering true can only make our C section SHORTER, which the
    ; subsequence relation permits by construction. The unit is unported either
    ; way, so the choice costs nothing and removes a way to be wrong.
    function check_grammar_variable_declaration_list(this, declaration_list: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if declaration_list = null
            return false
        let declarations AstNode.variable_declarations_of(declaration_list)
        if this.check_grammar_for_disallowed_trailing_comma(declarations, DiagTrailing_comma_not_allowed)
            return true

        if AstNode.list_count(declarations) = 0
        {
            if this.has_parse_diagnostics()
                return false
            var lpos 0
            var lend 0
            if this.list_range_of(declarations, &lpos, &lend) = false
            {
                this.record_unported("list-range", KindVariableDeclarationList)
                return false
            }
            return this.grammar_error_at_pos(lpos, lend - lpos, DiagVariable_declaration_list_cannot_be_empty)
        }

        let block_scope_flags AstNode.flags_of(declaration_list) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        var is_using false
        if block_scope_flags = NodeFlagsUsing
            set is_using: true
        var is_await_using false
        if block_scope_flags = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
            set is_await_using: true

        if is_using or is_await_using
        {
            let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(declaration_list)
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindForInStatement
            {
                if is_using
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_a_using_declaration)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_a_for_in_statement_cannot_be_an_await_using_declaration)
            }
            if (AstNode.flags_of(declaration_list) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
            {
                if is_using
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_await_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
            }
            if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindVariableStatement
            {
                let gk Checker.kind_or_unknown(AstNode.parent_node_of(parent))
                var in_clause false
                if gk = KindCaseClause
                    set in_clause: true
                if gk = KindDefaultClause
                    set in_clause: true
                if in_clause
                {
                    if is_using
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_case_or_default_clauses_unless_contained_within_a_block)
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(declaration_list, DiagX_await_using_declarations_are_not_allowed_in_case_or_default_clauses_unless_contained_within_a_block)
                }
            }
        }

        if is_await_using
        {
            this.record_unported("check-grammar-await-or-await-using", KindVariableDeclarationList)
            return true
        }

        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarForDisallowedBlockScopedVariableStatement, whole, and it is
    ; COMPLETE — nothing behind it is deferred, which is what lets the
    ; VariableStatement arm become the third of the switch's arms to run to its
    ; last line (the EmptyStatement and DebuggerStatement pair are the other two).
    ;
    ; ★★ IT ALWAYS ANSWERS FALSE. The reference's `return false` is not an
    ; oversight: this is the last term of checkVariableStatement's `&&` chain, its
    ; result is discarded, and the report it makes is a SEMANTIC error rather than a
    ; grammar one — `c.error`, not grammarErrorOnNode, so no parse guard. That is
    ; why it goes through error_on_node and why a file that failed to parse still
    ; gets a TS1156.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MESSAGE TAKES THE KEYWORD AS AN ARGUMENT AND THE FOUR ARMS COLLAPSE
    ; TO ONE CODE, which is check_grammar_modifiers' argument (§3.5ct) applied to a
    ; four-way switch: the dump is `C pos end code`, `let`/`const`/`using`/`await
    ; using` all produce TS1156, and a keyword string this port cannot print is a
    ; second thing to keep in step for no comparison. What is NOT dropped is
    ; upstream's `default: panic("Unknown BlockScope flag")` — the switch is over a
    ; masked value that is already known non-zero, so a fifth answer means the
    ; BlockScoped mask and the four constants have drifted apart, and the marker
    ; says so rather than reporting a diagnostic that looks right.
    ;
    ; ★★ AND THE MARKER IS UNREACHABLE FOR A REASON THAT IS WORTH WRITING DOWN,
    ; because it is not the usual *no corpus gets here*. The mask is three bits and
    ; the four constants cover 1, 2, 4 and 6 — so 3, 5 and 7 are the panic's
    ; population, and reaching one needs TWO of the bits set at once. The parser
    ; sets exactly one arm per list, and combined_node_flags ORs the list's flags
    ; with the STATEMENT's, which could add a bit if any OTHER flag used bits 0..2
    ; — and none does: NodeFlags.scaly's own header says those three positions are
    ; the variable-declaration aliases and nothing else. So this is a claim about
    ; the FLAG TABLE, and it expires if a flag is ever added at a low bit.
    ;
    ; ★ The combined flags come from the DECLARATION LIST, not from the statement —
    ; `let` sits on the list — and Binder.combined_node_flags walks up from there
    ; through the statement, OR-ing, which is why the same answer arrives whichever
    ; of the two the reference happens to hand it.
    function check_grammar_for_disallowed_block_scoped_variable_statement(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if this.container_allows_block_scoped_variable(AstNode.parent_node_of(node))
            return false
        let list AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
        if list = null
            return false
        let block_scope_kind Binder.combined_node_flags(list) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        if block_scope_kind = 0
            return false
        var known false
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsLet
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsConst
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
            set known: true
        if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
            set known: true
        if known = false
        {
            ; ★ The detail is the KIND and deliberately not the flag value, even
            ; though the flag is the interesting datum: tests/triage.py prints that
            ; column as a kind number, and NodeFlagsBlockScoped is 7, which is a
            ; real kind. A marker that reads as a wrong kind is worse than one that
            ; reads as a right one.
            this.record_unported("block-scope-flag-unknown", KindVariableStatement)
            return false
        }
        this.error_on_node(node, DiagX_0_declarations_can_only_be_declared_inside_a_block)
        false
    }

    ; containerAllowsBlockScopedVariable, whole. Seven kinds answer false — the
    ; six loop-and-branch statements whose body may be a single statement, plus
    ; `with` — and a LabeledStatement asks its own parent, which is the recursion
    ; upstream writes.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS ONE LINE IS WHAT SUPPRESSES TS1156 ON THE WHOLE CORPUS, and it is
    ; worth stating because the first draft of the control battery got it backwards.
    ; The caller RUNS on every variable statement — it is the last term of a chain
    ; slice 53 completes — so the report is not out of reach because the code is
    ; unexecuted; it is out of reach because every parent in reach (Block,
    ; ModuleBlock, SourceFile) falls through to the permissive answer.
    ; controls-slice53.sh's f10 was written expecting no change from making this
    ; answer false and came back RED 345; f11 gates the kind list itself. What no
    ; control can redden is the RECURSION: it would have to walk up to a refusing
    ; kind, and there is none within reach.
    ;
    ; ★ The parent is read null-safely for kind_or_unknown's reason: a
    ; VariableStatement at the top of a file has a SourceFile parent, but the
    ; recursion's own step can walk off the end of a label chain in a tree the
    ; parser built after an error, and KindUnknown falls out of every arm to the
    ; permissive answer the reference gives an unlisted kind.
    function container_allows_block_scoped_variable(this, parent: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        var p parent
        while true
        {
            let k Checker.kind_or_unknown(p)
            if k = KindIfStatement
                return false
            if k = KindDoStatement
                return false
            if k = KindWhileStatement
                return false
            if k = KindWithStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForInStatement
                return false
            if k = KindForOfStatement
                return false
            if k = KindLabeledStatement
            {
                set p: AstNode.parent_node_of(p)
                continue
            }
            return true
        }
        true
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarModifiers ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HISTOGRAM NAMED THIS ONE FUNCTION RATHER THAN ELEVEN ARMS. Slice
    ; 48's work list reads VariableStatement 3 940, ClassDeclaration 3 022,
    ; FunctionDeclaration 2 255, ImportDeclaration 1 601, InterfaceDeclaration
    ; 1 342, ModuleDeclaration 1 282, TypeAlias 699 on stage 2 — and
    ; checkGrammarModifiers is the first grammar check of every one of them, so
    ; nothing behind it can be reached without it. 356 lines of grammarchecks.go.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MESSAGE ARGUMENTS ARE NOT COMPUTED, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER
    ; THAN A SHORTCUT. Sixteen of the reports below are one of three parameterised
    ; messages — `{0} modifier already seen`, `{0} modifier cannot be used with
    ; {1} modifier`, `{0} modifier must precede {1} modifier` — which upstream
    ; fills from visibilityToString and scanner.TokenToString. A message's CODE
    ; does not depend on its arguments, and the dump is `C pos end code`: two
    ; diagnostics agreeing on those three numbers print identically. That is the
    ; same argument DiagnosticList.sort_by_position makes for stopping its
    ; comparison at the code, and it is why visibilityToString and TokenToString
    ; are not ported here. ★It is also the one thing that would have to change if
    ; a later yardstick compared TEXT — named here so that slice finds the bill
    ; instead of discovering it as a diff.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO COMPILER OPTIONS DECIDE WHETHER PARTS OF THIS FUNCTION EXIST AT ALL,
    ; and both are read off the harness the way slice 48 read AllowUnreachableCode.
    ; `c.legacyDecorators` is `ExperimentalDecorators == core.TSTrue` and the
    ; oracle sets neither, so it is FALSE — which makes the accessor-pair decorator
    ; check unreachable and makes NodeCanBeDecorated's parameter arm answer false
    ; before it asks anything. `VerbatimModuleSyntax` is likewise unset, so the
    ; CommonJS top-level-export test is dead. Both are written at their place with
    ; a report behind the option rather than omitted, because an OMITTED step is
    ; invisible and a reported one is not. ★This paragraph used to add *it
    ; additionally needs program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, which the stub program
    ; does not have*, and slice 54 measured that claim false: the oracle's program
    ; answers ModuleKindNone for it, which is a VALUE and not a hole (§3.5cy).
    function check_grammar_modifiers(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return false
        ; ★★ THE GUARD BELOW ASKS WHETHER *THIS CALL* REPORTED, not whether the
        ; checker has ever reported, and the difference is a whole file. Since
        ; slice 49 the statement loop no longer stops at the first unported arm
        ; (check_source_elements says why), so every statement after the first
        ; arrives here with the flag ALREADY set — and a blanket `is_unported()`
        ; test would return before checking any of them, silently, leaving
        ; diagcheck measuring one statement per unit. Measured on
        ; checker_modifier_already_seen.ts: one line instead of four.
        let unported_before this.is_unported()
        if this.report_obvious_decorator_errors(node)
            return true
        if this.report_obvious_modifier_errors(node)
            return true
        ; Either reporter may have met a construct this slice does not implement.
        ; Continuing would report the rest of the function's opinion about a node
        ; whose first question was never answered.
        if this.is_unported() <> unported_before
            return false

        if AstNode.is_this_parameter(node)
            return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagNeither_decorators_nor_modifiers_may_be_applied_to_this_parameters)

        ; blockScopeKind — read from the DECLARATION LIST, not from the statement:
        ; `using` and `await using` are flags of the list, and three arms below
        ; report on them.
        var block_scope_kind: int NodeFlagsNone
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindVariableStatement
        {
            let dl AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
            if dl <> null
                set block_scope_kind: AstNode.flags_of(dl) & NodeFlagsBlockScoped
        }

        var last_static null as pointer[AstNode]
        var last_declare null as pointer[AstNode]
        var last_async null as pointer[AstNode]
        var last_override null as pointer[AstNode]
        var first_decorator null as pointer[AstNode]
        var flags: int ModifierFlagsNone
        var saw_export_before_decorators false

        ; The reference's comment, kept because the whole leading/trailing
        ; distinction below is unreadable without it: *We parse decorators and
        ; modifiers in four contiguous chunks: [...leadingDecorators,
        ; ...leadingModifiers, ...trailingDecorators, ...trailingModifiers]. It is
        ; an error to have both leading and trailing decorators.*
        var has_leading_decorators false

        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let modifier *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            set i: i + 1
            if AstNode.is_decorator(modifier)
            {
                var grandparent null as pointer[AstNode]
                let dparent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
                if dparent <> null
                    set grandparent: AstNode.parent_node_of(dparent)
                if this.node_can_be_decorated(node, dparent, grandparent) = false
                {
                    var overload false
                    if AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindMethodDeclaration
                    {
                        if Binder.node_is_missing(AstNode.body_of(node))
                            set overload: true
                    }
                    if overload
                        return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagA_decorator_can_only_decorate_a_method_implementation_not_an_overload)
                    return this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
                }
                if legacy_decorators
                {
                    ; `else if c.legacyDecorators && (node.Kind == KindGetAccessor
                    ; || node.Kind == KindSetAccessor)`, whose body asks
                    ; GetAllAccessorDeclarationsForDeclaration — the symbol's
                    ; declaration list walked for the get/set PAIR — and reports
                    ; TS1207 on the second of them. Unreachable under this harness
                    ; (the option is unset, see the header), and reported rather
                    ; than dropped so that an options record arriving later turns
                    ; it into a port instead of into a silent wrong answer.
                    if Checker.is_accessor_kind(AstNode.kind_of(node))
                    {
                        this.record_unported("grammar-legacy-accessor-decorators", AstNode.kind_of(node))
                        return false
                    }
                }

                ; `flags&^(ExportDefault|Decorator) != 0` — *if we've seen any
                ; modifiers aside from `export`, `default`, or another decorator,
                ; then this is an invalid position*. Written as a SUBSET test
                ; because Scaly has no complement in this position and a
                ; complement constant would be a second thing to keep in step:
                ; the difference is non-empty exactly when flags is not a subset.
                if (flags & (ModifierFlagsExportDefault | ModifierFlagsDecorator)) <> flags
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)

                if has_leading_decorators
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsModifier) <> 0
                    {
                        ; The reference panics here if firstDecorator is nil — a
                        ; debug assertion, and it holds by construction:
                        ; has_leading_decorators is set only on the iteration that
                        ; also sets first_decorator.
                        ;
                        ; ★ Upstream spells the report out (hasParseDiagnostics,
                        ; c.error, AddRelatedInfo, return true / return false)
                        ; instead of calling grammarErrorOnNode, and the ONLY
                        ; difference is the related info — a second span attached
                        ; to the diagnostic, which ast.NewDiagnostic keeps on the
                        ; diagnostic rather than in the list, so it never reaches
                        ; a dump. What is left is grammarErrorOnNode exactly.
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagDecorators_may_not_appear_after_export_or_export_default_if_they_also_appear_before_export)
                    }
                }

                set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsDecorator

                ; if we have not yet seen a modifier, then these are leading
                ; decorators
                if (flags & ModifierFlagsModifier) = 0
                    set has_leading_decorators: true
                else
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                        set saw_export_before_decorators: true
                }

                if first_decorator = null
                    set first_decorator: modifier
            }
            else
            {
                let mk AstNode.kind_of(modifier)
                let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
                let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)

                ; Three placement tests that run before the switch, in the
                ; reference's order.
                if mk <> KindReadonlyKeyword
                {
                    if nk = KindPropertySignature
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_member)
                    if nk = KindMethodSignature
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_member)
                    if nk = KindIndexSignature
                    {
                        var index_ok false
                        if mk = KindStaticKeyword
                        {
                            if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                                set index_ok: true
                        }
                        if index_ok = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_index_signature)
                    }
                }
                var in_out_or_const false
                if mk = KindInKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if mk = KindOutKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if mk = KindConstKeyword
                    set in_out_or_const: true
                if in_out_or_const = false
                {
                    if nk = KindTypeParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_type_parameter)
                }

                ; NodeFlagsReparsed on the MODIFIER: a modifier the JSDoc reparser
                ; synthesized carries no source order, so every *must precede*
                ; test is skipped for it. Read once, because eleven arms ask it.
                let reparsed (AstNode.flags_of(modifier) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0

                if mk = KindConstKeyword
                {
                    if nk <> KindEnumDeclaration
                    {
                        if nk <> KindTypeParameter
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_class_member_cannot_have_the_0_keyword)
                    }
                    if nk = KindTypeParameter
                    {
                        var host_ok false
                        if AstNode.is_function_like_declaration(parent)
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindFunctionType
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindConstructorType
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindCallSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindConstructSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindMethodSignature
                            set host_ok: true
                        if host_ok = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_type_parameter_of_a_function_method_or_class)
                    }
                }

                if mk = KindOverrideKeyword
                {
                    ; The reference's note: *if node.kind === Parameter,
                    ; checkParameter reports an error if it's not a parameter
                    ; property.*
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsOverride
                    set last_override: modifier
                }

                var visibility false
                if mk = KindPublicKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if mk = KindProtectedKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if mk = KindPrivateKeyword
                    set visibility: true
                if visibility
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessibilityModifier) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAccessibility_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    ; ★ THE `else` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE ONE ARM OF THIS
                    ; FUNCTION THAT DOES NOT FLATTEN INTO INDEPENDENT TESTS.
                    ; Upstream's chain ends `else if flags&Abstract != 0 { if
                    ; private {…} else if !reparsed {…} } else if
                    ; IsPrivateIdentifierClassElementDeclaration(node) {…}` — so a
                    ; REPARSED non-private accessibility modifier beside `abstract`
                    ; falls out of the chain reporting NOTHING, and in particular
                    ; never reaches the private-identifier test. Every other arm
                    ; here is a run of returns and is equivalent flattened; this
                    ; one is not.
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                    {
                        if mk = KindPrivateKeyword
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if reparsed = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        if AstNode.is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(node)
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAn_accessibility_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | AstNode.modifier_to_flag(mk)
                }

                if mk = KindStaticKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_module_or_namespace_element)
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsStatic
                    set last_static: modifier
                }

                if mk = KindAccessorKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    if nk <> KindPropertyDeclaration
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_accessor_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_property_declaration)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAccessor
                }

                if mk = KindReadonlyKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsReadonly) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    ; The reference's note again: *if node.kind === Parameter,
                    ; checkParameter reports an error if it's not a parameter
                    ; property* — which is why Parameter is admitted here.
                    var readonly_host false
                    if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindPropertySignature
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindIndexSignature
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        set readonly_host: true
                    if readonly_host = false
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_readonly_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_property_declaration_or_index_signature)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsReadonly
                }

                if mk = KindExportKeyword
                {
                    ; The VerbatimModuleSyntax arm. Unset under this harness (see
                    ; the header), which is the whole of the hole — the claim that
                    ; program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile is a second one was
                    ; measured false in slice 54 (§3.5cy).
                    if verbatim_module_syntax
                    {
                        this.record_unported("grammar-verbatim-module-syntax", nk)
                        return false
                    }
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if reparsed = false
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                    {
                        if nk <> KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_class_elements_of_this_kind)
                    }
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsExport
                }

                if mk = KindDefaultKeyword
                {
                    var container parent
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) <> KindSourceFile
                        set container: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
                    var bad_container false
                    if Checker.kind_or_unknown(container) = KindModuleDeclaration
                    {
                        if Checker.is_ambient_module_or_null(container) = false
                            set bad_container: true
                    }
                    if bad_container
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagA_default_export_can_only_be_used_in_an_ECMAScript_style_module)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsExport) = 0
                    {
                        if reparsed = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    if saw_export_before_decorators
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(first_decorator, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsDefault
                }

                if mk = KindDeclareKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                    {
                        if nk <> KindPropertyDeclaration
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_class_elements_of_this_kind)
                    }
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_using_declaration)
                    if block_scope_kind = NodeFlagsAwaitUsing
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_an_await_using_declaration)
                    if (Checker.flags_or_none(parent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                    {
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagA_declare_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_already_ambient_context)
                    }
                    if AstNode.is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(node)
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAmbient
                    set last_declare: modifier
                }

                if mk = KindAbstractKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    var abstract_host false
                    if nk = KindClassDeclaration
                        set abstract_host: true
                    if nk = KindConstructorType
                        set abstract_host: true
                    if abstract_host = false
                    {
                        var member false
                        if nk = KindMethodDeclaration
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindGetAccessor
                            set member: true
                        if nk = KindSetAccessor
                            set member: true
                        if member = false
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_abstract_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_class_method_or_property_declaration)
                        var in_abstract_class false
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
                        {
                            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(parent, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
                                set in_abstract_class: true
                        }
                        if in_abstract_class = false
                        {
                            if nk = KindPropertyDeclaration
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAbstract_properties_can_only_appear_within_an_abstract_class)
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagAbstract_methods_can_only_appear_within_an_abstract_class)
                        }
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsPrivate) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        {
                            if last_async <> null
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_async, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                        }
                        if reparsed = false
                        {
                            if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAccessor) <> 0
                                return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                        }
                    }
                    if AstNode.is_private_identifier(AstNode.name_of(node))
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_a_private_identifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAbstract
                }

                if mk = KindAsyncKeyword
                {
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    var ambient false
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        set ambient: true
                    if (Checker.flags_or_none(parent) & NodeFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                        set ambient: true
                    if ambient
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_in_an_ambient_context)
                    if nk = KindParameter
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_parameter)
                    if (flags & ModifierFlagsAbstract) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_1_modifier)
                    set flags: flags | ModifierFlagsAsync
                    set last_async: modifier
                }

                var in_or_out false
                if mk = KindInKeyword
                    set in_or_out: true
                if mk = KindOutKeyword
                    set in_or_out: true
                if in_or_out
                {
                    var in_out_flag: int ModifierFlagsOut
                    if mk = KindInKeyword
                        set in_out_flag: ModifierFlagsIn
                    ; `node.Kind != TypeParameter || parent != nil && !(…)` — the
                    ; second half is guarded on a non-nil parent, so a type
                    ; parameter with no parent passes.
                    var wrong_host false
                    if nk <> KindTypeParameter
                        set wrong_host: true
                    if parent <> null
                    {
                        var variance_host false
                        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindInterfaceDeclaration
                            set variance_host: true
                        if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
                            set variance_host: true
                        if AstNode.is_type_or_js_type_alias_declaration(parent)
                            set variance_host: true
                        if variance_host = false
                            set wrong_host: true
                    }
                    if wrong_host
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_can_only_appear_on_a_type_parameter_of_a_class_interface_or_type_alias)
                    if (flags & in_out_flag) <> 0
                        return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_already_seen)
                    if (in_out_flag & ModifierFlagsIn) <> 0
                    {
                        if (flags & ModifierFlagsOut) <> 0
                            return this.grammar_error_on_node(modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_must_precede_1_modifier)
                    }
                    set flags: flags | in_out_flag
                }
            }
        }

        let nk AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if nk = KindConstructor
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsStatic) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_static, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsOverride) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_override, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_async, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_appear_on_a_constructor_declaration)
            return false
        }
        var import_kind false
        if nk = KindImportDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if nk = KindJSImportDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if nk = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            set import_kind: true
        if import_kind
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsAmbient) <> 0
                return this.grammar_error_on_node(last_declare, DiagA_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_with_an_import_declaration)
        }
        if nk = KindParameter
        {
            if (flags & ModifierFlagsParameterPropertyModifier) <> 0
            {
                if Binder.is_binding_pattern(AstNode.name_of(node))
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_property_may_not_be_declared_using_a_binding_pattern)
                if AstNode.dot_dot_dot_token_of(node) <> null
                    return this.grammar_error_on_node(node, DiagA_parameter_property_cannot_be_declared_using_a_rest_parameter)
            }
        }
        if (flags & ModifierFlagsAsync) <> 0
            return this.check_grammar_async_modifier(node, last_async)
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarAsyncModifier, whole. Four kinds may carry `async`; everything
    ; else that reached here reports at the modifier.
    function check_grammar_async_modifier(this, node: pointer[AstNode], async_modifier: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return false
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return false
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_node(async_modifier, DiagX_0_modifier_cannot_be_used_here)
    }

    ; reportObviousDecoratorErrors / findFirstIllegalDecorator. The pair is the
    ; DECORATOR half of the two questions ast.can_have_illegal_decorators exists
    ; to answer, and the port already carries the predicate (slice 24 wrote it for
    ; checkJSDecoratorSyntax): a node on that list may never carry a decorator, so
    ; the FIRST one it carries is reported wherever it sits.
    function report_obvious_decorator_errors(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let decorator Checker.find_first_illegal_decorator(node)
        if decorator = null
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(decorator, DiagDecorators_are_not_valid_here)
    }

    function find_first_illegal_decorator(node: pointer[AstNode]) returns pointer[AstNode]
    {
        if AstNode.can_have_illegal_decorators(node) = false
            return null
        Checker.find_first_decorator(node)
    }

    ; core.Find(node.ModifierNodes(), ast.IsDecorator) and its twin for
    ; ast.IsModifier. Two functions rather than one with a predicate parameter:
    ; Scaly's only escaping-closure form is `return \x: …` (§3.9e), and the port's
    ; standing answer to a callback argument is one function per call shape.
    function find_first_decorator(node: pointer[AstNode]) returns pointer[AstNode]
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return null
        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let m *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            if AstNode.is_decorator(m)
                return m
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    function find_first_modifier(node: pointer[AstNode]) returns pointer[AstNode]
    {
        let mods AstNode.modifiers_of(node)
        if mods = null
            return null
        let len mods.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            let m *(mods.get_buffer() + i)
            if Parser.is_modifier_kind(AstNode.kind_of(m))
                return m
            set i: i + 1
        }
        null
    }

    ; reportObviousModifierErrors / findFirstIllegalModifier / findFirstModifierExcept.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE THIRD GROUP OF findFirstIllegalModifier IS A `panic`, AND IT IS
    ; PORTED AS A REPORT. Upstream ends its inner switch with *Unhandled case in
    ; findFirstIllegalModifier* — reachable only for a node kind that carries
    ; modifiers, sits inside neither a module block nor a source file, and is none
    ; of the eight the switch names. None of the five kinds this slice's arms
    ; reach can be that node, so the arm is a guard for the next slice rather than
    ; a live path; a report is what keeps it from becoming a silent `nil`.
    function report_obvious_modifier_errors(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let modifier this.find_first_illegal_modifier(node)
        if modifier = null
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(modifier, DiagModifiers_cannot_appear_here)
    }

    function find_first_modifier_except(node: pointer[AstNode], allowed_modifier: int) returns pointer[AstNode]
    {
        let modifier Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if modifier = null
            return null
        if AstNode.kind_of(modifier) = allowed_modifier
            return null
        modifier
    }

    function find_first_illegal_modifier(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns pointer[AstNode]
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        ; Group one: the kinds whose modifiers are all legal here — the accessors,
        ; the members, the module and import/export elements, the two function
        ; expressions, a parameter, a type parameter and a JS type alias.
        if Checker.kind_has_no_illegal_modifier(k)
            return null
        ; Group two: the kinds that may carry NO modifier at all.
        if Checker.kind_admits_no_modifier(k)
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        ; The default group. A declaration at the top level of a file or of a
        ; namespace body is exempt outright; everything else is judged per kind.
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindModuleBlock
            return null
        if Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindSourceFile
            return null
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAsyncKeyword)
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAbstractKeyword)
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAbstractKeyword)
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        if k = KindVariableStatement
        {
            let dl AstNode.variable_statement_list_of(node)
            if dl <> null
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(dl) & NodeFlagsUsing) <> 0
                    return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindAwaitKeyword)
            }
            return Checker.find_first_modifier(node)
        }
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return Checker.find_first_modifier_except(node, KindConstKeyword)
        this.record_unported("find-first-illegal-modifier", k)
        null
    }

    function kind_has_no_illegal_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        if k = KindTypeParameter
            return true
        if k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    function kind_admits_no_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMissingDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.NodeCanBeDecorated. It lives here rather than in ast.scaly because two
    ; of its five arms ask HasAbstractModifier / HasAmbientModifier, i.e.
    ; Binder.has_syntactic_modifier — a method on the binder, since a node's
    ; modifier flags are computed rather than cached in this port. The checker is
    ; the object that holds a binder.
    function node_can_be_decorated(this, node: pointer[AstNode], parent: pointer[AstNode], grandparent: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        ; *private names cannot be used with decorators yet* — legacy only, and
        ; the option is unset here (see check_grammar_modifiers' header).
        if legacy_decorators
        {
            if AstNode.is_private_identifier(AstNode.name_of(node))
                return false
        }
        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        ; class declarations are valid targets
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        ; class expressions are valid targets for native decorators
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return legacy_decorators = false
        ; property declarations are valid if their parent is a class declaration
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
        {
            if parent = null
                return false
            if legacy_decorators
                return Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
            if Parser.is_class_like_node(parent) = false
                return false
            if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAbstract)
                return false
            return b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, ModifierFlagsAmbient) = false
        }
        ; if this method has a body and its parent is a class declaration, this is
        ; a valid target
        var method_like false
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            set method_like: true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            set method_like: true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            set method_like: true
        if method_like
        {
            if parent = null
                return false
            if AstNode.body_of(node) = null
                return false
            if legacy_decorators
                return Checker.kind_or_unknown(parent) = KindClassDeclaration
            return Parser.is_class_like_node(parent)
        }
        if k = KindParameter
        {
            ; The reference's TODO: *ParameterDeclaration decorator support for ES
            ; decorators must wait until it is standardized.* So the whole arm is
            ; legacy-only, and under this harness it answers here.
            if legacy_decorators = false
                return false
            ; What is left needs ast.GetThisParameter — the parent's first
            ; parameter, tested for being `this` — and the parent's body. Reported
            ; rather than written, for the reason the accessor-pair branch above
            ; is: unreachable while the option is unset, and a report is what turns
            ; the option's arrival into a port.
            this.record_unported("node-can-be-decorated-parameter", k)
            return false
        }
        false
    }
    ; ── the grammar check the file itself gets ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checkGrammarSourceFile, whole:
    ;
    ;   return node.Flags&ast.NodeFlagsAmbient != 0 &&
    ;       c.checkGrammarTopLevelElementsForRequiredDeclareModifier(node)
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 47 DEFERRED THE SECOND HALF ON ONE PREDICATE AND SAID SO, AND
    ; THIS IS THAT BILL PAID. `ast.IsDeclarationNode` is `node.DeclarationData()
    ; != nil` — a property of the node LAYOUT, not a list anyone can read off a
    ; switch — so its note read *a hand-written kind list here would be a silent
    ; wrong answer in both directions: a missing kind drops a TS1046 the reference
    ; reports, an extra one invents a diagnostic where a plain statement stands*.
    ; It is now DERIVED (DeclarationKinds.scaly, generated by closing the
    ; reference's struct-embedding graph over DeclarationBase and verified against
    ; the reference's own DeclarationData()), and the derivation is what shows how
    ; right the deferral was: seven of the fifty-six kinds are ones no reader would
    ; have guessed, BinaryExpression and ObjectLiteralExpression among them.
    function check_grammar_source_file(this) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(file) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return false
        this.check_grammar_top_level_elements_for_required_declare_modifier()
    }

    ; ★ The OR is the reference's own, and it is the proof that a VariableStatement
    ; is NOT a declaration node: upstream writes `ast.IsDeclarationNode(decl) ||
    ; decl.Kind == ast.KindVariableStatement`, which would be dead text if the
    ; predicate already admitted it. The derived table agrees — 244 is absent from
    ; it — so the two readings of the reference confirm each other.
    function check_grammar_top_level_elements_for_required_declare_modifier(this) returns bool
    {
        let stmts AstNode.statements_of(file)
        let n AstNode.list_count(stmts)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let decl AstNode.child_in_list(stmts, i)
            var considered AstNode.is_declaration_node(decl)
            if AstNode.kind_of(decl) = KindVariableStatement
                set considered: true
            if considered
            {
                if this.check_grammar_top_level_element_for_required_declare_modifier(decl)
                    return true
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    ; checkGrammarTopLevelElementForRequiredDeclareModifier. The reference's own
    ; comment on the grammar it enforces is worth keeping, because the exempt list
    ; below is not derivable from anything else:
    ;
    ;   A declare modifier is required for any top level .d.ts declaration except
    ;   export=, export default, export as namespace, interfaces and imports:
    ;
    ;     DeclarationElement:
    ;        ExportAssignment
    ;        export_opt   InterfaceDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   TypeAliasDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   ExternalImportDeclaration
    ;        export_opt   AmbientDeclaration
    function check_grammar_top_level_element_for_required_declare_modifier(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if Checker.kind_is_exempt_from_declare_modifier(AstNode.kind_of(node))
            return false
        var exempting: int ModifierFlagsAmbient | ModifierFlagsExport | ModifierFlagsDefault
        if b.has_syntactic_modifier(node, exempting)
            return false
        this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagTop_level_declarations_in_d_ts_files_must_start_with_either_a_declare_or_export_modifier)
    }

    ; The kind half of the condition above, as a function for the reason
    ; ast.scaly's is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node is one.
    function kind_is_exempt_from_declare_modifier(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ── checkGrammarStatementInAmbientContext ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The whole body of the EmptyStatement and DebuggerStatement arms and the first
    ; line of the ExpressionStatement one, which is why a slice aimed at the
    ; statement histogram starts here.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE ONCE-BIT IS THE POINT OF THE FUNCTION, not bookkeeping around it. The
    ; reference's own comment: *If we are in a block, we only want to really report
    ; an error once to prevent noisiness* — so the bit lives on the BLOCK for the
    ; second diagnostic and on the NODE for the first, and a port that dropped it
    ; would emit one TS1036 per statement of an ambient block instead of one per
    ; block. ★And the bit is set to the RESULT of the report, not unconditionally:
    ; when grammarErrorOnFirstToken is suppressed by a parse diagnostic, nothing is
    ; marked and the next statement asks again — which is the same answer, since
    ; the suppression is a property of the file rather than of the statement.
    ;
    ; ★ The else branch of the reference is an assertion it has commented out
    ; ("we must be parented by a statement ... our parent will have already done
    ; it"), so there is nothing to port there and the function falls through.
    function check_grammar_statement_in_ambient_context(this, node: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if (AstNode.flags_of(node) & NodeFlagsAmbient) = 0
            return false
        let parent AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if this.has_reported_ambient(node) = false
        {
            var in_signature AstNode.is_function_like(parent)
            if Checker.is_accessor_kind(AstNode.kind_of(parent))
                set in_signature: true
            if in_signature
            {
                let reported this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagAn_implementation_cannot_be_declared_in_ambient_contexts)
                if reported
                    this.mark_reported_ambient(node)
                return reported
            }
        }
        var in_block false
        let pk AstNode.kind_of(parent)
        if pk = KindBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindModuleBlock
            set in_block: true
        if pk = KindSourceFile
            set in_block: true
        if in_block
        {
            if this.has_reported_ambient(parent) = false
            {
                let reported this.grammar_error_on_first_token(node, DiagStatements_are_not_allowed_in_ambient_contexts)
                if reported
                    this.mark_reported_ambient(parent)
                return reported
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsAccessor, which is two kinds.
    function is_accessor_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        false
    }

    function has_reported_ambient(this, n: pointer[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let len reported_ambient.get_length() as int
        var i 0
        while i < len
        {
            if *(reported_ambient.get_buffer() + i) = n
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    procedure mark_reported_ambient(this, n: pointer[AstNode])
        reported_ambient.add(n)

    ; ── the diagnostics, as GetDiagnosticsForFile answers them ──────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE ONE THING THIS SLICE OWES THE NEXT ONE IS A SORT IT DOES NOT
    ; WRITE. GetDiagnosticsForFile sorts the collection before answering, so a port
    ; that emitted in DISCOVERY order would differ from the reference as a wrong
    ; SPAN on every line after the first — a difference that reads as a checker
    ; defect and is a missing sort. (tests/checktypes.py checks that invariant on
    ; the oracle's side for exactly this reason, and slice 46's c2 proves it can
    ; fail: 213 findings.) It is NOT written here because no arm of this slice
    ; produces a diagnostic — the list is empty in every unit — and a sort with
    ; nothing to sort is machinery no instrument can reach, which is the shape
    ; §3.5be argues against. The FIRST arm that reports owes it, and what it owes
    ; is written out so it is a transcription rather than a rediscovery:
    ;
    ;   CompareDiagnostics is path, then pos, then end, then code, then the message
    ;   ARGUMENTS and the message chain, and the sort is STABLE. The path is one
    ;   file here. The arguments cannot be compared — this port carries no message
    ;   text — and they cannot MATTER either: two diagnostics agreeing on pos, end
    ;   and code are the same three numbers in the dump, so any order between them
    ;   prints identically. That is the argument for stopping at the code, and it
    ;   is an argument rather than an approximation.
    function diagnostic_count(c: pointer[Checker]) returns int
        c.diagnostics.length()

    function diagnostic_at(c: pointer[Checker], i: int, out_pos: pointer[int], out_end: pointer[int], out_code: pointer[int]) returns bool
        c.diagnostics.at(i, out_pos, out_end, out_code)
}
